BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:9ad2bb3c9024144e4ab1f3c455ccc5902be1492e@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T095000
SUMMARY:Trust is Earned\, Verify Everyone: Impersonation Threats\, Global L
 aw\, and the ITSM Plumbing Behind Identity Proofing
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Commercial and Regulatory Imperatives\nSpeakers: Adam Ju
 revicius (Dropbox)\; Hugo Gabignon (Dropbox)\nOrganizations deploying pass
 keys and other phishing-resistant factors often skip: knowing who is actua
 lly authenticating.\n\nIn a remote-first hiring world\, with outsourced re
 sources and AI-assisted social engineering\, knowing who is behind the key
 board is no longer a safe assumption. Attackers exploit the gap between HR
  processes and verification methods.\n\nWhen impersonation attacks rose du
 ring the shift to virtual-first work\, we deployed identity proofing globa
 lly and tied it to high-risk workflows such as sensitive identity\, device
  and access changes. It was not simple.\n\nIdentity proofing at global sca
 le is not just a technical project. US employment law limits re-verificati
 on requirements without legal exposure. GDPR and country-specific privacy 
 rules shape how biometric data can be captured\, processed\, and retained.
  Co-employment and outsourced staffing models change what is permissible a
 nd enforceable. “Just use a phone” fails when smartphone access and de
 vice capability vary across countries and populations. These constraints b
 ecome the architecture.\n\nIn this session\, Hugo Gabignon and Adam Jurevi
 cius explain how we quantified risk using FAIR/ALE\; how legal\, privacy\,
  and operational realities shaped scope\; and why we chose a multi-vendor 
 approach because no single provider handles every jurisdiction well. We ma
 p decisions to NIST 800-63-3 and the Zero Trust Maturity Model\, then cove
 r pitfalls including data quality\, enrollment friction\, device requireme
 nts\, auditing\, and ITSM integration.\n\nYou’ll leave with a 5-step fra
 mework: Threat -> Scope -> Legal -> Technical -> Operational\, and an unde
 rstanding of why identity proofing is prerequisite infrastructure for phis
 hing-resistant authentication.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/
 authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Commercial and Regulatory Imperatives
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:e5b6f829a6907a7fa0bcc8581158b5747d210ab8@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T095000
SUMMARY:Modern Authentication Explained
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth\nSpeake
 rs: Aaron Woland (Duo)\nZero Trust is a principle\, not a product\; the sa
 me is true of Authentication\, Authorization and Accounting. The concepts 
 are universal and still apply when you refer to the “new” authenticati
 on protocols. Technologies like: OAuth\, SAML\, OIDC\, IAM\, IdP\, FIDO\, 
 Yubikeys\, Passkeys\, password-less\, MCP\, A2A\, AgenticAI\, DCR and many
  more. \n\nThis session will focus on explaining modern web-based authenti
 cation protocols\, learning the vocabulary and teaching it in a way that c
 ompares & contrasts them to network and security methods of old. In other 
 words: come learn about the latest in authentication protocols and how to 
 understand them to build a strong foundation for the other sessions at Aut
 henticate.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-202
 6/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:68e8257167351b54101586133a0c61da3238b0a1@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T095000
SUMMARY:Passkey Developer Office Hours: You’ve got questions\, we’ve pr
 obably got answers
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Matthew Miller (Cisco Du
 o)\; Tim Cappalli (Okta)\; Megan Shamas (MS Advisory)\nAre you a developer
  having trouble building out your support for passkeys because of all the 
 complexity in the ecosystem? Is there some browser footgun or platform UX 
 quirk that you can’t quite build around? Come get a consult from passkey
  subject matter experts! We’ll pass along some hard-earned guidance from
  our practical experiences implementing\, and driving the standards behind
 \, passkeys!\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2
 026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:4ae438c6348defb4b309a814871e7e4af8da55a5@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T090000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T104000
SUMMARY:Live! The Reality of Interoperable mDL Credentials
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Mobile and Digital Identity\nSpeakers: Chris Goh (Valid8
 )\; Ryan Williams (AAMVA)\; Eric Jorgensen (AstreaX)\; Paul Grassi (IDEMIA
  Civil Identity (CID))\; Bruce Hanson (Credence ID)\; Yash Shah (Credence 
 ID)\; Florent Tournois (France Titres)\; Anthony Carmoy (France Titres)\; 
 Jean-Baptiste Milan (HID Global)\; Rob White (Samsung Electronics America)
 \; Zachary Jones (Trinsic)\; Richard Grape (LexisNexis Risk Solutions)\nVe
 rifiable digital credentials and digital identity wallets promise to revol
 utionize the way we interact with online services\, and bridge the gap bet
 ween the physical and the digital in our increasingly connected world.\nBu
 t how close are we to realising this promise?\nModerated by digital creden
 tials luminary Chris Goh\, industry leaders from across sectors and nation
 s will demonstrate—live—the way that digital verifiable credentials wo
 rk today\, both online and in proximity. For authentication\, privacy-pres
 erving attribute verification\, cross-sector and cross-border business and
  more\, this session will equip you to take full advantage of the coming r
 evolution in credentials.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authe
 nticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Mobile and Digital Identity
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f812bb244acf8fab17bc78a6c33fec54220bd3fc@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T105000
SUMMARY:The Doppelgänger Protocol: Memory-Based Authentication in the Age 
 of Deepfakes
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication\nSpeakers: 
 Jonathan Bateman (Real Recognizes Real AI)\nA Ferrari executive stopped a 
 deepfake attack cold by asking one question: "What book did you recommend 
 to me two weeks ago?" That shared memory\, something no AI could replicate
 \, did what passwords\, 2FA\, and biometrics could not.\n\nAs voice clonin
 g and AI-generated video reach indistinguishability\, the authentication s
 tack we've spent decades building is showing its seams. Biometrics can be 
 spoofed. FIDO2 protects the credential\, not the human behind it. The ques
 tion this session asks is: what layer of verification sits above the proto
 col?\n\nThis talk introduces The Doppelgänger Protocol™: a cryptographi
 c framework that transforms shared human memories into unforgeable\, AI-re
 sistant authentication keys. \n\nWhat you'll learn:\n\nWhy deepfake attack
 s expose the gap between credential security and identity assurance\nHow m
 emory-based challenges create cryptographic proof of shared human experien
 ce\nThe technical architecture: local key generation\, challenge protocols
 \, and privacy preservation\nHow The Doppelgänger Protocol layers on top 
 of–rather than replacing– existing authentication standards\n\n\nWhat 
 you'll do:\nAfter a focused technical breakdown\, you'll pair up with anot
 her attendee\, create shared memory challenges\, and experience the protoc
 ol firsthand seeing how two strangers can establish a cryptographically ve
 rified trusted connection in minutes through a mobile-first experience. Yo
 u'll leave with open-source code to examine\, fork\, and build upon.\nWalk
  away with: A new mental model for human-layer authentication\, hands-on p
 rotocol experience\, and a framework for thinking about where memory-based
  verification fits alongside FIDO\, passkeys\, and existing identity infra
 structure.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-202
 6/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:8ad1fb1243757717e444587bf09c226415c41c8b@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T105000
SUMMARY:Identity for AI is hard... Fortunately Authorization doesn't have t
 o be - Practical access control for MCP & AI Agents
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth\nSpeake
 rs: Mark Berg (Axiomatics)\nFiguring out identity and authentication for A
 I processes\, agents\, and other non-human identities is hard. One might a
 sk what kind of token should we issue an agent. What scopes should they be
  given\, etc? Fortunately authorization for AI\, be it MCP or agents\, is 
 relatively straightforward. In this masterclass\, we will cover the challe
 nges AI brings with regards to access control such as:\n- overcoming the l
 imitations of role-based access control\n- solving the static claims probl
 em\n- introducing constrained access delegation\, and\n- implementing poli
 cy as consent.\n\nRuntime access control\, as embodied by NIST ABAC 800-16
 2 and its Zero Trust sibling\, 800-207\, enables enterprises to roll out A
 I in a controlled and secure way leveraging defense-in-depth principles:\n
 - MCP servers and gateways act as natural enforcement points that can reac
 h out at runtime to policy decision points over OpenID AuthZEN.\n- Agents'
  access can be constrained through dynamically-generated claims and consen
 t-as-policy constructs\n- RAG can be dynamically filtered to achieve the r
 ight levels of control.\n\nThis masterclass will propose a multi-faceted a
 rchitecture that provides a comprehensive approach to securing AI within t
 he enterprise. We will tackle\n- functional access control: can a user or 
 agent even access a given tool?\n- transactional access control: can a use
 r or agent act on a given record?\n- data-centric access control: which re
 cords can an agent or user retrieve?\n\nAttendees will walk away with a cl
 ear\, standards-based blueprint they will be able to apply in their enterp
 rise.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:feea9997b0230c185a882c5a624511ac7be2331f@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T100000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T105000
SUMMARY:Grade your passkey implementation from Bronze to Gold
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Mitchell Galavan (Google
 )\; James Hwang (Microsoft)\nThe era of passkey research has evolved into 
 the era of passkey navigation. Join the UXWG as we launch the 2026 Impleme
 ntation Rubric—a self-serve tool to reduce "time-to-confidence" for RPs.
  We’ll demonstrate how to use this scorecard to optimize today’s flows
  while mapping the next frontier: using Digital Credentials to solve the "
 lost device" problem and defining the "human-in-the-loop" for the coming a
 ge of AI Agents.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u
 -s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:7cf735bb57b6b62ce045bc551004c01ab563a078@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T115000
SUMMARY:600 million users\, one login: Spotify's impossible authentication 
 challenge
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication\nSpeakers: 
 Quendoline Jansen (Spotify)\; Laura de Bella (Spotify)\nThe biggest vulner
 ability in your authentication system is not technical. It is the assumpti
 on that your users are all the same.\n\nMost authentication systems are bu
 ilt with one user in mind. A tech savvy adult\, on a modern device\, with 
 stable internet\, in a market where passkeys just work. At Spotify\, that 
 user is a fiction.\n\nSpotify’s 600+ million users span every continent\
 , device\, and level of digital literacy. From a child on a family plan wi
 thout an email address\, to a visually impaired user navigating with a scr
 een reader\, to a first time smartphone user in an emerging market\, to an
  artist managing their career. These are not edge cases. At this scale\, t
 hey are the mainstream. Building authentication for all of them is not jus
 t a design challenge. It is a security and business challenge.\n\nIn this 
 Viewpoint session\, two members of Spotify’s IAM team explore a question
  rarely asked at authentication conferences: what happens to your security
  model when your user is literally everyone?\n\nFriction that signals secu
 rity to an engineer can feel like exclusion to a user who cannot complete 
 a verification step. A login flow optimised for one market can create acco
 unt duplication in another. A single global standard can leave millions be
 hind.\n\nSpotify did not solve this by lowering the security bar. Instead\
 , we challenged the assumption that security and user experience are in te
 nsion. By aligning product\, engineering\, and design\, we reframed IAM as
  a growth and usability function\, and built an authentication approach th
 at works for more people without compromising system integrity.\n\nThis se
 ssion will challenge you to examine your own authentication stack and ask:
  who are you actually leaving behind?\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com
 /event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1327f9eac23b407683b133dad345171384eb8ab6@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T115000
SUMMARY:AI Agent Identity Patterns with SPIFFE and SPIRE
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Non-human Authentication\nSpeakers: Brendan Paul (Amazon
  Web Services)\; Swara Gandhi (Amazon Web Services)\nAI agents are becomin
 g autonomous participants in enterprise systems\, calling APIs\, accessing
  databases\, and invoking tools across cloud and on-premises environments.
  But how do you authenticate a process that isn't a human and isn't a trad
 itional service? Static API keys and shared secrets don't scale\, and they
  fall short of zero-trust requirements when agents dynamically chain tool 
 calls across trust boundaries.\n\nThis talk explores how SPIFFE and SPIRE 
 bring cryptographic\, short-lived workload identity to AI agent runtime en
 vironments. We'll cover how to issue SPIFFE identities to agents without d
 istributing long-lived credentials. We'll then show how to allow the agent
  to access resources autonomously\, as well as how to combine agent identi
 ty with user identity to build fine-grained authorization. \n\nAttendees w
 ill walk away understanding how to apply workload identity standards to AI
  agents\, bridge human and non-human authentication\, and implement author
 ization models that account for the delegation patterns of agentic AI.\nAg
 enda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Non-human Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:357f209dc648550156f0efe8205b8906484d3319@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T115000
SUMMARY:Tests are good\, actually! Exploring the WebAuthn Virtual Authentic
 ator API
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Matthew Miller (Cisco Du
 o)\nThe WebAuthn spec defines a robust user agent automation API. Whether 
 they’ve built or bought\, Relying Parties can use WebAuthn’s virtual a
 uthenticator feature to create and run automated passkey support tests usi
 ng real browsers. Let’s explore what it takes to make the most of this c
 apability\, with an eye on leveraging it to gain and maintain confidence i
 n our collective support for passkeys.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.co
 m/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:803bdc14959712cee0f2438c1d57176ee1ede669@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T115000
SUMMARY:Everything You Wanted to Know About FIDO’s New Initiatives\, But 
 Were Afraid to Ask
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Masterclass\nSpeakers: Diego Zavala (FIDO Alliance)\; Ni
 shant Kaushik (FIDO Alliance)\nIf you still think of the FIDO Alliance onl
 y as the organization behind passkeys\, then this masterclass is for you. 
 The FIDO Alliance updated its mission last year and is extending its work 
 across the digital identity ecosystem\, with initiatives spanning verifiab
 le digital credentials\, payments (including agentic payments)\, digital w
 allets\, agentic AI\, secure device onboarding with FDO\, and the next gen
 eration of passkey capabilities of course.\nThis session provides a guided
  tour of these initiatives and\, more importantly\, explains how they fit 
 together to address some of the ecosystem’s biggest challenges around sc
 alability\, interoperability\, security\, and user choice. Whether you kno
 w FIDO primarily through passkeys or follow its standards work closely\, t
 his is an opportunity to learn about the bigger picture we're operating in
  and understand what FIDO is building for the next phase of trusted digita
 l identity.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-20
 26/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Masterclass
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:79bcf91b3df85b64e3a1896dadff81417ff812ee@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T134500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T141000
SUMMARY:From Policy to Passkeys: A Practical Playbook for Enterprise Adopti
 on
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Tanu Kaushik (ebay inc)\
 nEnterprises often understand the security value of passkeys and phishing-
 resistant MFA\, but many still struggle to move beyond pilots into sustain
 ed workforce adoption. This session proposes a practical roadmap for enter
 prise programs that need to improve enrollment\, reduce support burden\, a
 nd build executive confidence. This session will give enterprise security 
 and identity teams a concrete\, repeatable roadmap to increase passkey ado
 ption. Rather than focusing only on architecture\, it will show how succes
 sful programs combine sponsorship\, simple enrollment\, onboarding integra
 tion\, communications sequencing\, and operational metrics to move from pi
 lot to scale.\n\nThe outcome is a reusable adoption playbook for enterpris
 es that are stuck with low enrollment and fragmented change management.\n\
 nWhy this session matters\nMany enterprises fail not because passkeys do n
 ot work\, but because adoption is treated as a pure technology rollout ins
 tead of a product\, communications\, and change-management program.\nAgend
 a: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:0943d124cb440540924ce579405a890adc1e5f07@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T134500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T141000
SUMMARY:Closing the Seams: Architectural Patterns for Post-Authentication T
 rust
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth\nSpeake
 rs: Samuel Folkes (Amazon Web Services (AWS))\nPasskeys solve the authenti
 cation problem. But what happens after login? Across organizations a patte
 rn keeps repeating: phishing-resistant authentication at the front door\, 
 followed by a session cookie that can be stolen\, a recovery flow that fal
 ls back to SMS\, or an authorization decision that never re-evaluates risk
 . Attackers don't need to beat your passkey. They just find the weakest se
 am downstream. This session introduces architectural patterns for closing 
 the trust gaps between authentication and everything that follows. Drawing
  on real-world identity architecture decisions\, we will walk through four
  critical seams and the open standards that address each:\n\n1. Authentica
 tion to Session Binding. How Device Bound Session Credentials (DBSC) and t
 oken binding prevent the "steal the cookie" attack class that makes AiTM p
 roxies effective even against FIDO2.\n\n2. Session to Continuous Evaluatio
 n. How CAEP and the Shared Signals Framework (SSF) allow identity provider
 s and relying parties to revoke sessions in near-real-time when device pos
 ture changes\, impossible travel is detected\, or a credential is compromi
 sed.\n\n3. Authentication to Account Recovery. Eliminating knowledge-based
  recovery without creating lockout risk\, covering multi-credential regist
 ration strategies\, recovery passkey patterns\, and verifiable digital cre
 dentials as a recovery path that doesn't re-introduce phishable factors.\n
 \n4. Initial Trust to Ongoing Authorization. Architecting authorization la
 yers that reference real-time identity signals rather than making static d
 ecisions at login time\, connecting FIDO2 assurance levels to context-awar
 e authorization.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u
 -s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:0c7cb2dc4704f617f1d22cf9e5af6d065b98fe46@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T134500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T141000
SUMMARY:Rise of the ID Wallets
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Mobile and Digital Identity\nSpeakers: Pedro Martinez (T
 hales)\nThey've been underground for a while. Now they are rising to the s
 urface. All around the world\, we can see Digital ID Wallets popping out n
 ow: \n- In Europe\, regulation introduced in 2024 mandates all 27 EU membe
 r states\, (population 450M)\, to release Digital Identity Wallets (EUDIW)
  for their citizens by the end of 2026. And by mid 2027\, public and comme
 rcial services will have to accept EUDIW as a valid form of identification
  for their users. \n- In the US\, 15+ states covering 3/4 of the US popula
 tion have or are implementing mobile driving license (mDL) projects\, with
  projections of close to 150M mDLs issued by 2030. \n- In India\, the Aadh
 ar Digital ID is set to cover a population of 1B. \n- Other large projects
  in countries like Brazil\, UK\, Japan\, ... confirm that the trend to ID 
 Wallets is global\, and that they will be part of our daily interactions i
 n the digital world before we know it. They will change the way we interac
 t online with services\, with the promise of greater security and more pri
 vacy control for end users.\n\nIn this session we will explore how our lif
 e as users will change as soon as ID Wallets become mainstream. Will we fe
 el safer? Will our experience as users improve or become more cumbersome? 
 Will we feel more in control of our data? Will it lead us to do more onlin
 e? What will we use them for? Will we use multiple wallets or will we choo
 se to put our digital IDs into one single aggregator wallet? Besides gover
 nments\, who else will issue DCs and what for?\n\nWe will explore early de
 ployments and large scale pilots to look for lessons learn and most promis
 ing use cases.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s
 -2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Mobile and Digital Identity
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:4105e245ad9b75a0a34f1b539496ca8a518945d1@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T134500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T141000
SUMMARY:Say Hello to PXP\, FIDO’s Newest (Sort of) Standard
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication\nSpeakers: 
 Nishant Kaushik (FIDO Alliance)\; Tim Cappalli (Okta)\nOriginally develope
 d to enable cross-device authentication for passkeys\, the CTAP Hybrid pro
 tocol has evolved into the Proximity Exchange Protocol (PXP)\, a more gene
 ral framework for secure\, proximity-based credential exchange between dev
 ices. PXP can support passkey experiences as well as the issuance and pres
 entation of verifiable digital credentials\, including through a new peer-
 to-peer offline transport that works without Internet connectivity.\nThis 
 session will unpack how PXP works\, why it evolved\, and the range of use 
 cases it can enable across authentication and digital identity. With proto
 col walkthroughs\, demonstrations\, and Q&A\, attendees will leave with a 
 practical understanding of where PXP fits and what it can do.\nAgenda: htt
 ps://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:8054cc17e3d632912a3babedba757cb10fd09ce0@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T142000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T144500
SUMMARY:Passkeys for Singpass - A Tale of Scaling Secure Login Across Singa
 pore
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Samantha Li (Government 
 Technology Agency of Singapore (GovTech Singapore))\nAs scams become more 
 sophisticated\, strong authentication needs to be both phishing-resistant 
 and practical for everyday users. Singpass\, Singapore’s national digita
 l identity platform\, chose to implement passkeys to give Singapore reside
 nts a safer and simpler way to authenticate.\nThis session will share Sing
 pass’s journey from deciding to adopt passkeys to introducing them to us
 ers at national scale. It will cover the product\, rollout and user adopti
 on considerations involved in reaching beyond early adopters\, including h
 ow to build trust\, support different levels of digital confidence\, and m
 ake a new authentication method feel familiar. The session will also share
  lessons from the adoption process and practical considerations for organi
 sations introducing passkeys to a broad user base.\nAgenda: https://authen
 ticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cf5b1d4b3b06591a95c01e9de462412b6f220c77@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T142000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T144500
SUMMARY:Unifying Zero-touch Onboarding
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth\nSpeake
 rs: Richard Kerslake (FIDO Alliance)\; Geof Cooper (FIDO Alliance)\; Gerar
 do Diaz Cuellar (Microsoft)\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/aut
 henticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bd9e07663e345b729b1a9025e4b76473d37096f8@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T142000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T144500
SUMMARY:I Am Not My Digital Identity! Or am I?
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Mobile and Digital Identity\nSpeakers: Christine Abruzzi
  (Cacapon Cyber Solutions LLC)\nFrom the day we are provisioned onto this 
 planet\, until the day our corporeal credentials expire\, we accumulate ta
 ngible artifacts of our existence. Revision 4 of NIST’s Special Publicat
 ion 800-63A\, Digital Identity Guidelines: Identity Proofing and Enrollmen
 t\, guides CSPs\, RPs\, and other organizations on incorporating some of t
 hese artifacts – known as identity evidence – into their identity proo
 fing and onboarding processes. \n\nUsing the presenter’s own life journe
 y as a case study\, this 25-minute session takes a deeper look at the role
  identity evidence plays in the proofing and onboarding processes\, includ
 ing the concepts of identity attributes\, evidence strength\, evidence and
  attribute validation sources\, and alternative identity proofing pathways
  for those individuals who do not possess the required evidence for a give
 n identity assurance level (IAL). \n\nIn addition to providing a review of
  these concepts and challenges\, this presentation will also serve as a ge
 ntle reminder that digital identity professionals just happen to be real p
 eople too.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-202
 6/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Mobile and Digital Identity
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:910ede741f9c29766cbabdff02a9d7b8198286b7@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T142000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T144500
SUMMARY:Passkeys Meet Policy: Extending Your IAM Controls with the FIDO Met
 adata Service
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Karen Larson (FIDO Allia
 nce)\; Rolf Lindemann (OneSpan)\nYou already write policies about who can 
 access what\, from which devices\, and under what conditions. When you rol
 l out passkeys\, a new question appears: what kind of authenticator is act
 ually holding that credential? As passkeys become the default way in which
  people sign in\, new questions arise for enterprises and “any passkey" 
 isn't always good enough due to considerations regarding compliance or ent
 erprise policies. Security teams need to know which authenticator is behin
 d a credential: Is it certified? Is it hardware-bound? Has a vulnerability
  been reported against it since it was deployed?\nThe FIDO Metadata Servic
 e (MDS) helps to close that gap. It's a public\, continuously updated cata
 log of authenticators using vendor provided data to tell you who made an a
 uthenticator\, if that authenticator is FIDO Certified\, and whether any s
 ecurity problems have been reported against an authenticator. With MDS fee
 ding your identity platform\, authenticator characteristics become one mor
 e attribute your policies can evaluate\, alongside user role\, device post
 ure\, and risk signals.\nIn this session we'll cover:\n\n• How MDS works
 : how attestation lets you identify an authenticator\, explained in plain 
 terms.\n• Writing policy: using MDS data for rules such as "admins must 
 use certified\, device-bound keys" or "allow synced passkeys for low-risk 
 apps."\n• Ongoing monitoring: keeping policy current as authenticators a
 re certified\, updated\, or flagged.\n• Trade-offs: common pitfalls\, an
 d how to balance strong assurance with a smooth user experience.\nYou'll l
 eave with a practical model for treating authenticator trust as part of yo
 ur IAM policy from day one of your passkey implementation and practical wa
 ys of implementing policy for existing passkey implementations.\nAgenda: h
 ttps://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fcfb7bf444ae3b4afba133b5fe0188049b3504e9@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T145500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T152000
SUMMARY:Hardware-Rooted Zero Trust: Cryptographic Attestation for Managed D
 evice Identities
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth\nSpeake
 rs: Venkatesh Gopal (Meta Platforms\, Inc)\nZero trust demands continuous 
 verification\, yet most enterprises still authenticate employee devices wi
 th phishable credentials — leaving a critical gap in the "never trust\, 
 always verify" model for access to internal infrastructure. This talk pres
 ents Meta's multi-year journey to close that gap by building cryptographic
  attestation into employee device identity — using hardware-bound keys i
 n TPMs and Secure Enclaves to provide unforgeable proof of device trust.\n
 \nWe describe a four-stage zero trust maturity model for device identity: 
 perimeter-based trust\, credential-based enrollment (phishing-vulnerable)\
 , hardware-bound keys (Trust on First Use)\, and full cryptographic attest
 ation with key provenance. We detail cross-platform implementations: TPM 2
 .0 attestation with Endorsement and Platform Certificates on Windows/Linux
  (leveraging Intel's Transparent Supply Chain program and Lenovo collabora
 tions)\, and Apple Managed Device Attestation via ACME on macOS and iOS. O
 ur implementation leverages open-source foundations — Smallstep's step-c
 a for ACME certificate issuance and Google's go-attestation for TPM attest
 ation — allowing us to focus on platform integration and fleet-scale ope
 rations. Critically\, we show how attestation signals feed directly into z
 ero trust access policy decisions — making device identity a first-class
  input alongside user identity.\n\nWe share operational lessons from deplo
 ying across Meta's 100K+ employee devices\; vendor platform certificate av
 ailability challenges \, TPM memory constraints requiring transient keys\,
  macOS certificate renewal conflicts with Apple's MDM lifecycle\, enrollme
 nt gaps and more.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-
 u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:b403c9d0da1cb1f6aa59c36a38b98b6c5e65a06b@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T145500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T152000
SUMMARY:Getting Digital Identity Wrong: Possible Problems and Why they Matt
 er
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Mobile and Digital Identity\nSpeakers: Brent Zundel (Yub
 ico)\nA frank look at possible issues with digital identity technologies. 
 Qualities of an ideal digital identity system are explored and gaps identi
 fied between the ideal and the reality. Includes issues such as user priva
 cy\, issuer and verifier integrity\, data minimization difficulties\, and 
 the role of trust.\nFinally\, some recommended guidelines for determining 
 whether we're on the right track.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/eve
 nt/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Mobile and Digital Identity
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:41a44ee0d994324e2bcdbefadf77fb71c4218f3c@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T145500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T152000
SUMMARY:Certification: The Foundation of Trust
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Paul Heim (FIDO Alliance)\nOpen standards make intero
 perability possible\, but certification is what makes them dependable. As 
 digital identity ecosystems mature\, the missing ingredient is no longer t
 echnology\, but confidence. Certification provides independent assurance t
 hat implementations conform to standards\, interoperate consistently\, and
  meet defined security and privacy requirements. It transforms specificati
 ons into trusted products that organizations can confidently deploy and re
 ly on.\nThis session explores why certification is the critical enabler fo
 r global adoption\, and describes FIDO Alliance efforts underway that elev
 ate certification above mere compliance into a catalyst for ecosystem grow
 th.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:770100dbef49f410e377e0cd6cb2dd28d54244ff@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261019T180000
SUMMARY:Opening Keynotes
LOCATION:Sol 1 - 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, C
 arlsbad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Keynote\nSpeakers: Andrew Hindle (Authenticate)\; Andrew
  Shikiar (FIDO Alliance)\; Christiaan Brand (Google)\; Matt Charpentier (J
 P Morgan Chase)\; Pamela Dingle (Microsoft)\nWelcoming Remarks | Andi Hind
 le\, Conference Chair\nOpening Keynote: Real People\, Trusted Agents | And
 rew Shikiar\, Executive Director & CEO\, FIDO Alliance\nRinging in the Cha
 nges | Christiaan Brand\, Principal Product Manager\, Google\nAn On-ramp t
 o the Future | Matt Charpentier\, Managing Director\, Head of Identity at 
 JPMorgan Chase and Andrew Shikiar\, Executive Director & CEO\, FIDO Allian
 ce\nNew Authentication Boundaries for New Futures | Pam Dingle\, Director 
 of Identity Standards\, Microsoft\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/eve
 nt/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Keynote
