BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:c0a87bb81012a7709d01e66635778a8821eb66c0@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T103000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T105500
SUMMARY:From Account Takeovers to Growth: Rethinking Authentication at Spot
 ify
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Commercial and Regulatory Imperatives\nSpeakers: Laura d
 e Bella (Spotify)\nIAM work is usually seen as important\, but it’s rare
 ly treated as a priority. Even when it’s driving real business problems\
 , it tends to lose out to feature work.\n\nAt Spotify\, our IAM team was s
 tuck in that pattern. We were dealing with account takeovers\, high custom
 er support costs\, and a backlog of foundational work that never made it o
 nto the roadmap.\n\nThis talk is about what changed\, and what didn’t.\n
 \nInstead of pushing harder on security and compliance arguments\, we star
 ted framing IAM in terms of growth: sign-up and login aren’t just securi
 ty layers\, they’re the entry point to the entire user experience and a 
 primary growth vehicle. That shift changed how we prioritised work\, how w
 e talked to leadership\, and what we were able to get funded.\n\nI’ll co
 ver:\n- How we connected IAM problems to user growth\, retention\, and sup
 port costs in a way that actually resonated with the rest of the business\
 n- What changed when we moved from a squad-based model to a value stream a
 pproach\, and where it created unexpected friction\n- How a small team mov
 ed from reacting to requests to setting a long-term direction (and where t
 hat broke down)\n- What impact we saw on user experience and operational l
 oad\, and what’s still unresolved\n\nThis session is for IAM\, security\
 , and platform teams trying to get foundational IAM work prioritised. It i
 sn’t a framework or a success story - it’s a look at what it took to m
 ake identity work visible\, valuable\, and worth investing in.\nAgenda: ht
 tps://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Commercial and Regulatory Imperatives
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:9b6e9ef96d623552b53c7d9730c0463c6617f884@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T103000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T105500
SUMMARY:Introduction to Access Management
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth\nSpeake
 rs: Lance Peterman (Dick's Sporting Goods)\nAccess management sits at the 
 center of every modern identity system\, yet many newcomers arrive at Auth
 enticate without a solid foundation to build on. This session gives first
 ‑time attendees a practical\, jargon‑free introduction to how access a
 ctually works: the core concepts\, the moving parts\, and the real-world p
 atterns that shape today’s IAM programs. Through relatable examples and 
 industry-tested guidance\, participants will walk away with the mental mod
 el they need to make sense of the rest of the conference—and to engage c
 onfidently in conversations about policies\, protocols\, and architecture.
 \nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:e05c0129f2988c70a7c767965a34433aae33d5e7@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T103000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T105500
SUMMARY:Navigating the Road to a Passwordless Enterprise: Lessons from the 
 GM Journey
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Andrew Cameron (General 
 Motors)\; Nick Lewallen (General Motors)\nRetrofitting passwordless authen
 tication into GM’s complex hybrid enterprise proved to be a journey full
  of unexpected twists and valuable insights. This session shares hard-won 
 lessons and best practices from our experience migrating a massive user ba
 se to cloud-native authentication\, all while maintaining seamless access 
 to essential on-premise applications.\nWe’ll highlight the critical proc
 esses and strategies that enabled us to realize the true benefits of Zero 
 Trust—including continuous verification\, least privilege\, and device h
 ealth checks—across a hybrid landscape. Attendees will gain practical gu
 idance on:\n\n• Identifying and overcoming common roadblocks in legacy e
 nvironments\n• Building a strong foundation for Zero Trust adoption\n•
  Unifying identity governance across cloud and on-premise systems\n• Ens
 uring user experience and operational continuity during migration\nJoin us
  for an experience-based deep dive\, offering actionable takeaways and a b
 lueprint for modernizing enterprise identity security—no matter how comp
 lex your environment.Continuous Identity architectures enable a move away 
 from static models of authorization (RBAC\, ABAC\, etc.) to models where r
 eal-time context can be used and Zero Standing Privilege is at the core of
  how authorization is granted. We will deep dive into the Four Components 
 of Modern Identity (Policy\, Orchestration\, Execution\, and Data) require
 d to build an identity fabric capable of handling millions of daily access
  decisions without latency-inducing bottlenecks.\nJoin us to learn how to 
 transition your IAM authorization infrastructure from a reactive\, bottlen
 ecked legacy environment into a proactive\, event-driven ecosystem system 
 that enforces security at the speed of business\, based on Zero Trust prin
 ciples.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:eb6375d5df508134d16109e6ae9c746f9f087fb5@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T103000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T105500
SUMMARY:How the Right MFA Can Impact Cyber Insurance Coverage
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Jeremy Grant (Venable LLP)\nCyber insurers have a lon
 g history of influencing the security practices of the organizations they 
 cover – in that the right security controls can play a big role in reduc
 ing risk and impacting the cost of insurance premiums. As the pace of phis
 hing attacks increases – and generative AI makes phishing attacks more s
 ophisticated and scalable – cyber insurers are now realizing that many o
 f the legacy MFA methods they previously promoted are no longer sufficient
 .\n\nThis session will discuss a new paper from the Center for Cybersecuri
 ty Policy and Law that dives into these issues\, and calls on cyber underw
 riters to take four steps to help drive the transition from legacy MFA to 
 phishing-resistant MFA. It will detail what the Center learned through hol
 ding several roundtables with key players in the cyber insurance industry\
 , and outline how encouraging the use of phishing-resistant MFA can help u
 nderwriters more accurately segment risk\, reduce preventable losses\, and
  steer the market toward controls that reflect the realities of an AI-enab
 led threat environment.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authent
 icate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f1ab879b5691219c403b9ace5e671ba02ba84007@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T110500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T113000
SUMMARY:From FIDO to Fines: Avoiding Regulatory Privacy Bite Marks
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Commercial and Regulatory Imperatives\nSpeakers: Jamie D
 anker (Google Cloud)\; Christine Owen (1Kosmos)\nThis fireside chat explor
 es how rapidly evolving privacy and data protection laws are reshaping the
  commercial deployment\, design choices\, and risk profile of modern IAM p
 rograms\, with a focus on biometrics\, identity verification\, passkeys\, 
 and emerging digital identity wallets. We will unpack key legal and regula
 tory themes across CCPA\, Illinois’ BIPA\, GDPR\, and emerging framework
 s in China and India\, highlighting lawful basis\, consent\, data minimiza
 tion\, cross-border data transfers\, and data subject/consumer rights as t
 hey apply to biometric\, identity\, and wallet credential lifecycles. Buil
 ding on this legal foundation\, we will translate regulatory expectations 
 into concrete implementation and governance patterns\, showing how organiz
 ations can tailor technical controls\, data flows\, and oversight mechanis
 ms to satisfy regulators while still delivering scalable\, user-friendly\,
  phishing-resistant passkey sign-ins. Attendees will gain a clear view of 
 the global privacy landscape and actionable guidance to reduce legal and r
 egulatory exposure while enabling sustainable business adoption of passkey
 s\, digital identity wallets\, and other FIDO-aligned solutions. Clearly w
 e will address the 900 pound gorilla in the room – privacy during the Ag
 entic AI revolution.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authentica
 te-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Commercial and Regulatory Imperatives
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2f808ceae9bb01a24c035b29880a91d5428b86cd@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T110500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T113000
SUMMARY:Standards to Secure Access Beyond Authentication: SSF\, CAEP\, Auth
 Zen\, and Transaction Tokens
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth\nSpeake
 rs: Atul Tulshibagwale (CrowdStrike)\nAuthentication is only the beginning
 . In today's threat landscape\, verifying identity at the point of login i
 s necessary but no longer sufficient. Attackers exploit the gap between in
 itial authentication and ongoing access — hijacking sessions\, abusing o
 ver-privileged tokens\, and moving laterally across systems long after cre
 dentials have been validated.\n\nA new generation of open standards is clo
 sing that gap. This session explores four complementary specifications tha
 t together form a foundation for continuous\, context-aware\, and least-pr
 ivilege access:\n\n- Shared Signals Framework (SSF): Provides the reliable
  infrastructure to communicate information asynchronously across organizat
 ional and vendor boundaries in near real-time\n\n- Continuous Access Evalu
 ation Profile (CAEP): Built on SSF\, CAEP enables relying parties to respo
 nd immediately to session anomalies\, credential changes\, and risk signal
 s without waiting for token expiry\n\n- AuthZen: Is an emerging standard f
 or interoperable\, fine-grained authorization\, decoupling policy decision
 s from application logic and enabling dynamic access control.\n\n- Transac
 tion Tokens (TraTs): TraTs are short-lived\, workload-scoped tokens that c
 arry rich context through service-to-service calls that enable zero-trust 
 enforcement deep inside microservices architectures by locking down identi
 ty and authorization context in call chains.\n\nAttendees will leave with 
 a clear understanding of how these standards interoperate\, where they are
  in their adoption lifecycle\, and how to start implementing them today to
  build access control systems that are dynamic\, auditable\, and resilient
  against modern identity-based attacks.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.c
 om/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:05a79c0a22ce72164d45440f59936fe157772171@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T110500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T113000
SUMMARY:Balancing Security & UX: The Hidden Trade-offs in Modern Authentica
 tion
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Sumanth Pinninti (Tinder
 )\; Manish Chhabra (Tinder)\nAuthentication sits at the intersection of se
 curity\, user trust\, and business growth but improving one often creates 
 unexpected consequences for the others. Stronger controls can prevent frau
 d and account takeovers\, yet they can also introduce friction\, increase 
 abandonment\, and lock legitimate users out of their accounts. Meanwhile\,
  reducing friction may improve conversion while creating new opportunities
  for abuse.\nIn this session\, Manish Chhabra and Sumanth Pinninti will ex
 plore the hidden trade-offs involved in designing authentication experienc
 es at scale. Drawing on practical lessons from SMS verification\, federate
 d sign-in\, passkeys\, risk-based challenges\, and account recovery\, he w
 ill discuss how product and security teams can make better decisions witho
 ut treating security and user experience as opposing goals.\nAttendees wil
 l learn how to evaluate authentication changes using both security and cus
 tomer metrics\, identify unintended consequences before a broad rollout\, 
 and use experimentation and progressive enforcement to balance protection 
 with accessibility. The session will provide a practical framework for bui
 lding authentication systems that are secure\, intuitive\, and resilient w
 hile preserving the trust of legitimate users.\nAgenda: https://authentica
 tecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:0d084350da8170f89e9d5ba34a0adbcd232ef666@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T110500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T113000
SUMMARY:Mind the Gap: Have Passkeys Crossed the Chasm?
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Rew Islam (Dashlane)\; T
 ina Zhuo (Dashlane)\nPasskey adoption has been climbing for years\, champi
 oned by platform giants and security advocates alike. But "crossing the ch
 asm" means something specific: the Early Majority is using passkeys not be
 cause they were told to\, but because they expect to. So where does the da
 ta actually put us in 2026?\n\nThis session interrogates that question usi
 ng anonymous\, aggregated authentication signals observed across thousands
  of relying parties and millions of users\, providing a cross-platform vie
 w no single RP or platform can provide. We'll examine whether passkey and 
 password usage profiles are converging\, which industries have moved beyon
 d Big Tech into the mainstream\, and what the adoption curves from 2024 to
  2026 actually look like.\n\nWe'll also look at what worked: how automatic
  upgrades\, conditional creation\, and related origins show up as observab
 le inflection points in real-world usage data. We'll also examine early si
 gnals from credential portability to see where the industry stands on adop
 tion.\n\nKey takeaways:\n• A data-grounded answer to: have passkeys cros
 sed the chasm\, and for whom?\n• Where passkey growth has stabilized\, a
 ccelerated\, or surprised us\n• How leading RPs' technical investments (
 mandates\, auto-upgrades\, conditional mediation) show up in observable us
 er behavior\n• What the data says about the remaining gap and who still 
 needs to cross it\nRPs leave knowing how to prioritize passkey investments
 : which technical additions moved the needle\, which verticals are pulling
  ahead\, and where user friction remains.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon
 .com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:f1a2a1a981e7cc57071be0961d541617f1c580f2@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T114000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T123000
SUMMARY:Presented by Google: Beyond SMS: Next Generation Phone Number Verif
 ication
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Masterclass\nSpeakers: Laila Gai (Google)\; Malcolm Deck
  (Google)\nThe passwordless revolution is underway\, yet traditional phone
 -based onboarding remains stuck in the era of high-cost\, high-friction\, 
 and insecure SMS OTPs. Telephony fraud\, such as Artificial Inflation of T
 raffic (AIT)\, along with high friction-induced drop-off rates (~15-20% in
  emerging markets) continue to tax developers looking to secure their user
  bases. This masterclass introduces a paradigm shift: next-generation\, si
 lent Firebase Phone Number Verification (PNV) powered by modern carrier ne
 twork APIs (TS.43/CAMARA)\, combined with a sneak preview of the upcoming 
 unified Sign in with Google (SiWG) layered identity bundle. Through live\,
  on-device demonstrations and deep-dive code walk-throughs\, we will show 
 you how to leverage cellular carrier channels to verify physical SIM cards
  silently over WiFi or cellular connections in under 4 seconds. We’ll co
 mpare standalone Firebase PNV with the upcoming SiWG integrated flow\, wal
 k through developer SDK implementations\, and map out a future-proof mobil
 e identity roadmap.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticat
 e-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Masterclass
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:714a09dc82dfc1255d549ded48b1c7552eee88e6@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T114000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T123000
SUMMARY:Presented by Mastercard: Digital Payment Credentials: Beyond Passke
 ys\, Redefining the Checkout Experience
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Masterclass\nSpeakers: Prateek Dwivedi (Mastercard)\nDig
 ital Payment Credentials (DPC) are redefining how trust\, authentication\,
  and payment converge in digital commerce. This masterclass explores how p
 ayments can evolve beyond passkeys toward Digital Payment Credentials and 
 how DPCs can support a broad range of payment and commerce journeys. Throu
 gh real-world examples and live demonstrations\, participants will learn h
 ow DPCs can be provisioned and used for payment authentication\, payment i
 nitiation\, combined payment and identity validation\, and emerging agenti
 c commerce use cases. The session will also examine how initiatives such a
 s the European Digital Identity Wallet (EUDI Wallet) are accelerating the 
 convergence of digital identity\, wallet technologies\, and existing payme
 nt infrastructure\, creating new opportunities to meet regulatory requirem
 ents while transforming the checkout experience. Attendees will gain a pra
 ctical understanding of the technologies\, standards\, and ecosystem devel
 opments driving this evolution\, and discover how Digital Payment Credenti
 als have the potential to redefine checkout and enable the next generation
  of trusted digital commerce\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/au
 thenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Masterclass
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:df4ce75de35a6928d1812e15359f3a35ffd1dae9@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T114000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T123000
SUMMARY:Presented by Yubico: Beyond the Passkey: Enhancing the Account Life
 cycle
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Masterclass\nSpeakers: Cody Salas (Yubico)\; Alan Alvare
 z (Yubico)\nYou’ve successfully secured your users with a passkey\; now 
 what? While passkeys are the gold standard for providing phishing-resistan
 t authentication\, critical gaps remain across the account lifecycle: How 
 do you perform secure account recovery? How do you streamline enrollment a
 t scale? How does identity assurance fit into your passkey strategy?\n\nIn
  this session Yubico will discuss new technical components\, consideration
 s\, and user flows that can be used to expand your passkey solutions inclu
 ding:\n\n• How to use biometric and document-based identity verification
  to strengthen recovery scenarios.\n• How utilizing a variety of enrollm
 ent mechanisms streamline passkey registrations.\n• How a mixed authenti
 cator strategy provides access to all corners of your user base.\n\nThis s
 ession is perfect for technical leaders supporting passkey solutions and a
 re seeking a beginner-level overview of concepts like identity verificatio
 n\, enrollment tools\, and authenticator management.\nAgenda: https://auth
 enticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Masterclass
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dc41da32e1972db38caf6a9c9fae3778942b8e9f@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T114000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T123000
SUMMARY:Authentication at the Next Frontier: Building Post Quantum Trust fo
 r Passkeys and Phishing-Resistant Authentication Architectures
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication\nSpeakers: 
 PreetiOhri Khemani (Infineon)\; Sarah Lefavrais (THALES)\; Beatrice Peiran
 i (Thales)\; Bassam Al-Khalidi (Axiad)\nThe arrival of cryptographically r
 elevant quantum computing is no longer a distant concern\, it is shaping a
 rchitectural decisions being made today.\n\nAs the identity ecosystem acce
 lerates passkey adoption\, a critical question emerges: how do we ensure t
 hat authentication systems deployed now will remain trustworthy in a post 
 quantum world?\n\nThis interactive viewpoint session brings together compl
 ementary perspectives - semiconductor\, hardware\, authentication platform
  and credential lifecycle management - to explore how post quantum cryptog
 raphy (PQC) will impact authentication and identity systems in practice.\n
 \nSpeakers will discuss current progress in post quantum cryptography stan
 dardization\, how the FIDO Alliance is preparing specifications for quantu
 m resilience\, and why crypto agility and hybrid cryptography are becoming
  foundational design requirements.\n\nFrom silicon anchored roots of trust
  and hardware bound certificate based authenticators and passkeys\, to pla
 tform level orchestration and credential lifecycle management\, the discus
 sion will examine how organizations can introduce quantum resistance while
  preserving usability\, interoperability\, and scale.\n\nThis session aims
  to address the practical design choices identity architects\, security le
 aders\, and product teams must make today to prepare authentication system
 s for the post quantum era.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/aut
 henticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fe46d9ca0961d2f562d2255b24e405bc0f720a01@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T123000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T133000
SUMMARY:New Login\, Who Dis? | Spirited Conversations by IPQS
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Alexander Hall (IPQS)\; Dennis Weiss (IPQS)\; Laura d
 e Bella (Spotify)\; Nishant Kaushik (FIDO Alliance)\nFive billion passkeys
  are now in use worldwide. Fraud hasn't slowed down\, because the login wa
 s never the whole account. Over lunch\, Spotify's Laura de Bella\, FIDO Al
 liance CTO Nishant Kaushik\, and IPQS fraud strategist Alexander Hall take
  the builder's\, the standards\, and the attacker's side of the same quest
 ion.\nSponsored by IPQS\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authent
 icate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ddb3a054be93b93961d39fb7a4c663964e67713e@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T142500
SUMMARY:Passkeys at Work: Research\, Reality\, and the Road to Workforce-Sc
 ale Deployment
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Philip Corriveau (RSA)\n
 Enterprises are no longer debating whether passkeys work — they are navi
 gating how to deploy them across diverse workforces\, devices\, and securi
 ty requirements. This session combines the latest FIDO Alliance workforce 
 and enterprise research with production-tested case studies to help organi
 zations move from pilot programs to deployment at scale.\n\nWe'll share cu
 rrent findings from the FIDO Alliance Enterprise Deployment Working Group\
 , including recent U.S. and UK research showing that a majority of surveye
 d organizations have already deployed or are actively rolling out passkeys
  for employee sign-ins. Most are adopting a blended strategy that combines
  device-bound and synced passkeys — and reporting measurable improvement
 s in security posture\, user experience\, productivity\, and help-desk cos
 ts. We'll pair these findings with insights from the FIDO Passkey Index to
  illustrate what "good" looks like in practice\, covering eligibility\, en
 rollment\, authentication success rates\, and operational impact.\n\nThe s
 econd half grounds this research in real enterprise experience. Through de
 tailed case studies — including RSA's own journey toward workforce-wide 
 passwordless authentication — we'll examine the decisions that most infl
 uence success: workforce segmentation\, assurance-based policy design\, en
 rollment and recovery hardening\, and UX patterns that guide users toward 
 valid authentication paths rather than dead ends. These examples make clea
 r that there is no single workforce and no single passkey exper\nAgenda: h
 ttps://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:e6840388061e29073483572c6d2f65c53b6f736c@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T142500
SUMMARY:What is Trust vs Identity - and why understanding the difference de
 fines our Architecture
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Mobile and Digital Identity\nSpeakers: Chris Goh (Valid8
 )\nHaving now operated and worked across North America\, Europe\, Asia and
  the Pacific\, it is clear our understanding of trust and identity are oft
 en disparately different. Those differences drive how authentication\, bin
 ding and overall security controls are defined\, regulated and designed. \
 n\nThis presentation aims to separate these key themes and look to underst
 and these topics and the architecture that is adopted based on those under
 standings. It will talk about the role of Verifiable Credentials\, in part
 icular mDocs\, authentication and the role of Passkeys\, but more importan
 tly\, beyond protocols what is the underlying trust architecture that need
 s to be protected.\n\nWith the oncoming advancements of AI and post Quantu
 m technology\, trust and how its earned\, delegated and applied is more im
 portant than ever regardless of protocol. We need to ensure the conversati
 on transcends protocols and is grounded in trust. Without that\, regardles
 s of our product stacks\, the efficiency or widespread adoption of our pro
 tocols\, we risk jeopardizing the security and privacy identity is used fo
 r - people.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-20
 26/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Mobile and Digital Identity
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:5fdf05f2bad0b64dd1a125b3f7fe81777b40d924@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T142500
SUMMARY:Presented by Arculus: Who Said Yes? Proving the Human Behind the AI
  Agent
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Non-human Authentication\nSpeakers: Abhishek Bhattachary
 a (Arculus)\nAgentic commerce is in production. Google's AP2\, Mastercard'
 s Verifiable Intent and Visa Intelligent Commerce each let an AI agent car
 ry a payment credential and act on a user's instruction\, and in April 202
 6 the FIDO Alliance formed an Agentic Authentication Technical Working Gro
 up so that a verified human stands behind every agent acting on their beha
 lf.\nSo when the agent books the flight or pays the invoice\, who said yes
 \, and can the issuer prove it? Every framework shipping today authenticat
 es the instruction. None yet standardizes how to attest which enrolled hum
 an approved it\, from an authenticator the agent cannot reach.\nThis sessi
 on starts with what passkeys already fix for financial institutions\, then
  follows the authentication moment off the checkout page and onto the mand
 ate. It shows how a FIDO2 credential on the payment card an issuer already
  ships can serve as the human-in-the-loop approval for high-consequence ag
 ent actions. One tap binds amount\, payee and scope into a device-bound si
 gnature and leaves a dispute artifact behind. It closes with what Secure P
 ayment Confirmation and PSD2 dynamic linking already cover\, where they st
 op\, and what the working groups still have to settle.\nAgenda: https://au
 thenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Non-human Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:077e14ad8de560cdc3a941fd3ae6de4cf3d0a1b7@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T142500
SUMMARY:Presented by Transmit Security: The Agent Behind the User: Identity
  and Trust When Your Customers Delegate to AI
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Non-human Authentication\nSpeakers: Raj Galagali (Transm
 it Security)\nAI agents are already logging in and transacting inside cust
 omer-facing applications on behalf of real users\, breaking the core assum
 ption that a credential belongs to the human presenting it. This session l
 ays out what identity teams must do before enforcing any policy: detect th
 at an actor is an agent\, classify which kind\, trace the delegation chain
  back to the human behind it\, and infer intent from behavior. We'll consi
 der how emerging standards work can extend the phishing-resistant foundati
 ons of FIDO and passkeys to delegated\, non-human actors\, and why visibil
 ity comes first. Attendees leave with a practical framework for extending 
 identity and trust to agents.: AI agents are already logging in and transa
 cting inside customer-facing applications on behalf of real users\, breaki
 ng the core assumption that a credential belongs to the human presenting i
 t. This session lays out what identity teams must do before enforcing any 
 policy: detect that an actor is an agent\, classify which kind\, trace the
  delegation chain back to the human behind it\, and infer intent from beha
 vior. We'll consider how emerging standards work can extend the phishing-r
 esistant foundations of FIDO and passkeys to delegated\, non-human actors\
 , and why visibility comes first. Attendees leave with a practical framewo
 rk for extending identity and trust to agents.\nAgenda: https://authentica
 tecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Non-human Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:5baa98ec736685f99b310b97b8b7fa2cf3b534b9@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T143500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T150000
SUMMARY:How Microsoft is Making Passkeys Available to Everyone
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Bob Gilbert (Microsoft)\
 ; Rakia Segev (Microsoft)\nMicrosoft is continuing its push toward a fully
  passwordless future\, with passkeys at the center of that strategy. This 
 session highlights the latest progress in Microsoft’s passkey journey\, 
 including new Microsoft Password Manager capabilities—expanded support i
 n Microsoft Edge on mobile\, native passkey integration in Windows 11\, an
 d secure passkey sync for Entra ID users.\n\nWe’ll also look under the h
 ood at Windows platform security enhancements that enable user-bound secre
 ts. Attendees will get an overview of recent Windows Virtualization‑Base
 d Security (VBS) Enclave SDK updates that allow applications to bind crede
 ntials or other secrets to Windows Hello user verification\, unlocking new
  patterns for protecting sensitive data\, enforcing user presence\, and de
 livering hardware‑isolated security guarantees.\nAgenda: https://authent
 icatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2a54b1001f70adf28e54f4f4b48e921013e26d40@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T143500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T150000
SUMMARY:Passkeys at Scale: Lessons from Capital One's Consumer Launch\, Ado
 ption and Trust for Millions
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Nidhin Benjamine (Capita
 l One)\; Dhaval Shah (Capital One)\; Sujeet Sinha (Capital One)\nCapital O
 ne shipped consumer passkeys. That was day zero\, the easy part. The real 
 journey started after launch: turning a technically working feature into s
 omething millions of customers\, across every device and OS combination\, 
 actually choose to use all under bank-grade risk and regulatory constraint
 s that don't apply to social or e-commerce platforms.\nThis session shares
  Capital One's end-to-end passkey journey as a top-5 U.S. bank rolling out
  passwordless authentication at consumer scale. We'll cover how we sequenc
 ed rollout from pilot to full availability under strong customer authentic
 ation requirements\, and the hurdles we didn't expect device and credentia
 l-provider fragmentation (iCloud Keychain\, Google Password Manager\, Wind
 ows Hello). We'll also cover a factor many passkey case studies skip: many
  of our customers are unfamiliar with passkeys and hesitant to trust authe
 ntication without a password\, which shaped an onboarding approach built f
 or a mainstream banking population rather than early adopters.\nAvailabili
 ty alone didn't move adoption. Usage stayed in the single digits until we 
 shifted from a passive experience to auto-prompting eligible customers at 
 sign-in\, ahead of introducing autofill and conditional UI on the web\, a 
 sequencing decision that did more for adoption than any change to the unde
 rlying implementation. We'll walk through that shift and the results it pr
 oduced\, and what it took to retire weaker fallback methods without lockin
 g customers out.\nAttendees will leave with a practical playbook for drivi
 ng passkey adoption\, designing recovery as a fraud control\, and managing
  the messy middle between "it works" and "customers use it" at a scale and
  risk profile most passkey case studies don't touch.\nAgenda: https://auth
 enticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:e2a4f02106582f3b434515512a9dc82a92903fff@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T143500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T150000
SUMMARY:Presented by Glide Identity: Identity at the Crossroads: Four Quest
 ions on the Road Past Phishable Login
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Mobile and Digital Identity\nSpeakers: Rakan Khalid (Pay
 Pal)\nPasskeys proved that strong security and a great user experience can
  coexist. So why do so many sign-ins still end in a code someone can phish
 ? Rakan Khalid\, Product Lead at PayPal\, and Amit Bhatia\, Product Lead a
 t Intuit\, join Eran Haggiag\, CEO of Glide ID\, to ask: Where should iden
 tity be? What's in the way? What happens if we miss? What are we doing abo
 ut it? Eran will also put a proposal on the table: 2σ27\, an open challen
 ge to make phishable authentication rare by the end of 2027. It's a goal w
 ith coin-flip odds even if everyone pushes\, and the panel will test wheth
 er it's realistic. Bring your roadmap.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.co
 m/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Mobile and Digital Identity
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2ddeada2bbf5925951bae232081a1ebc0be220f9@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T143500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T150000
SUMMARY:Presented by Twilio: Inside the MCP Roadmap: What's Next for Agenti
 c Identity
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Non-human Authentication\nSpeakers: Max Gerber (Twilio)\
 ; Den Delimarsky (Anthropic)\nAs Model Context Protocol (MCP) adoption gro
 ws\, security and trust take center stage. Join Anthropic and Twilio to ex
 plore MCP’s roadmap for authentication\, authorization and token securit
 y. We’ll look at Twilio’s own MCP tools\, connectors and agent skills 
 for building verification and fraud prevention with AI coding tools—and 
 what the evolving standard means for Twilio and other teams building with 
 MCP.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Non-human Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:b724bdc4a9124ca74a2491a5ab341877d909af21@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T151000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T153500
SUMMARY:The Recovery Paradox: Solving the Passkey Recovery Crisis Without a
  Downgrade
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Debayan Basu (Genuine Pa
 rts Company)\nPasskeys were introduced as the "Password Killer\," promisin
 g an end to phishing and credential stuffing. But in our rush to deploy\, 
 we’ve so far ignored a dangerous irony: the "Reset Passkey" button has b
 ecome the new "Reset Password" button. For many organizations\, the recove
 ry path for a lost or unusable passkey still relies on the very legacy MFA
  — SMS and email passcodes that passkeys were meant to replace. This cre
 ates a "Security Downgrade" vulnerability that attackers are already explo
 iting.\n\nThis session moves beyond the "Happy Path" of passkey creation t
 o the "Crisis Path" of passkey recovery. We will analyze real-world "war s
 tories" where phishing-resistant high-assurance passkey deployments were b
 ypassed via their recovery flows. We will explore a blueprint for building
  a "Circular Trust" model — leveraging ecosystem sync\, identity proofin
 g\, and "High-Friction" recovery delays — to ensure that when a user los
 es their device\, they don’t lose their security. We’ll bridge the gap
  between "impossible to phish" and "impossible to recover\," proving that 
 a passkey is only as secure as the method used to reset it.\nAgenda: https
 ://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:726721b30071e2b1a9a4c63038ed35d0bc2e22ee@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T151000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T153500
SUMMARY:Wiring Digital Identity Wallets into Payment Authentication: SCA\, 
 Dynamic Linking\, and the Industry Transformation Nobody Fully Planned For
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Mobile and Digital Identity\nSpeakers: Mallikarjuna Pott
 a (PayPal)\; Mahendar Madhavan (PayPal)\neIDAS 2.0 mandates that banks\, P
 SPs\, and payment wallets accept the European Digital Identity Wallet (EUD
 IW) as a Strong Customer Authentication method by November 2027. But EUDIW
  based authentication is not just a new authentication method — it is go
 ing to be a structural change to every layer of the payments stack\, and m
 ost of the industry has not yet reckoned with what that means end-to-end.\
 nThis session addresses two interlocked technical problems. First: why PID
  alone cannot satisfy PSD2's RTS requirements\, and why a dedicated Paymen
 t Wallet Attestation — device-bound\, PSP-issued\, pre-registered before
  first use — is the only compliant path. Second: how dynamic linking\, t
 he cryptographic binding of a wallet's signed proof to a specific amount\,
  currency\, and payee\, must propagate end-to-end across issuer-captured (
 OOB/3DS)\, merchant-captured\, and pay-by-bank flows simultaneously.\nDraw
 ing on EWC and WeBuild large-scale pilot experience\, EMVCo 3DS integratio
 n work\, and live PSP implementation across card and account payment scena
 rios\, we map the full authentication data chain — from PWA issuance thr
 ough OID4VP presentation\, through EMV 3DS AReq transport\, to ACS validat
 ion — and identify exactly where specifications leave gaps that will cau
 se implementation failures before 2027.\nWe then step back to examine what
  "Pay with EUDIW" does to the checkout surface across every actor in the p
 ayment chain\, and what analogous pressures are building in the US\, India
 \, and beyond. The 2027 mandate is an EU problem today and a global archit
 ecture question tomorrow.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authe
 nticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Mobile and Digital Identity
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:34e28f10c41aa8de2679bbe67909779c9d81ab5a@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T151000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T153500
SUMMARY:Presented by Cisco Duo: Why Active Directory Remains Your Biggest A
 uthentication Risk
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Aaron Woland (Duo)\nPasskeys are reshaping authentica
 tion\, but 90% of the Fortune 1000 still rely on Active Directory—and id
 entity debt has made it an attacker’s opportunity. Unowned service accou
 nts\, dormant identities\, excessive privilege\, identity drift and legacy
  Kerberos/NTLM flows create blind spots modern controls miss. Cisco Talos 
 found that 44% of identity-based attacks targeted AD. In this session\, le
 arn a practical detect-and-defend approach: extend identity security postu
 re management to on-prem AD\, prioritize cleanup using cross-platform risk
  and attack-path context\, then close hard-to-cover access gaps by enforci
 ng MFA at the domain controller—without rewriting legacy applications. L
 eave with a roadmap to defend the identity foundation your organization st
 ill depends on.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-
 s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:5bb8f10bc77d542f3c20b13008634579ba0bbb13@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T151000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T153500
SUMMARY:Presented by Ping Identity: Zero Knowledge Biometrics: The Future o
 f Authentication
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Ali Nazem (Ping Identity)\; Dalton Slattery (Ping Ide
 ntity)\nThe world is moving beyond knowledge-based checks\, call centers\,
  and key fobs. The next evolution is biometric authentication that truly p
 roves a user is who they claim to be by matching them to the verified digi
 tal identity established during IDV or employee onboarding. With Zero-Know
 ledge Biometrics\, authentication becomes seamless: a single glance at the
  camera authenticates the verified user in under a second across workforce
  and CIAM use cases\, including onboarding\, login\, step-up authenticatio
 n\, and account recovery. This is enabled by a unique cryptographic approa
 ch that ensures biometric data is never stored in a retrievable or reconst
 ructable form\, on either the server or device. The result is secure\, pri
 vacy-preserving authentication that delivers stronger protection and effor
 tless user experiences.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authent
 icate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:b01c6febda2f5a0bb0e4a4c2ea977cb963d2c324@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T162500
SUMMARY:Unlocking the Future: Inside Meta’s Passkey Strategy
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Alistair Thornton (Meta)
 \; Jason Cheung (Meta)\nMeta is building the future of human connection an
 d the technology that makes it possible. Meta’s apps serve billions of u
 sers globally and include Facebook\, Instagram\, WhatsApp\, and Meta AI.\n
 \nSince launching passkeys on WhatsApp in 2024 and Facebook/Messenger in J
 une 2025\, Meta is moving forward ambitiously with passkeys across our fam
 ily of apps. Learn how Meta is addressing the unique challenges of deployi
 ng passkeys at scale\, improving the passkey experience on Meta’s apps\,
  and advancing the overall authentication ecosystem.\nAgenda: https://auth
 enticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:544299c7c47957c132b97701c142740f53c0881a@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T162500
SUMMARY:Trusting the Bots: Identity and Authorization in the Age of AI Agen
 ts
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Non-human Authentication\nSpeakers: Zack Martin (Venable
  LLP)\; Ryan Galluzzo (NIST)\nThis fireside chat with official(s) from the
  National Cybersecurity Center of Excellence will dive into the progress o
 f the project that is looking at identity and authorization of agentic AI.
 \nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Non-human Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:3eda0054332282ee47eeab703bf9137e2f1c5332@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T162500
SUMMARY:Authenticated Isn't Authorized: Gating High-Risk Agent Actions When
  the Agent Holds a Valid Passkey
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication\nAs AI agen
 ts become more autonomous\, they will naturally possess the credentials th
 ey need to authenticate\, such as valid tokens or passkeys. However\, just
  because an AI agent can successfully log into a system doesn't mean the h
 uman user actually authorized the specific action the AI is about to take.
  The agent might be acting on misunderstood instructions\, or it may have 
 been manipulated by a malicious prompt injection. If a service provider ex
 ecutes a high-risk action based solely on the agent's credentials\, it ris
 ks executing an unauthorized task—and carries the liability if things go
  wrong.\nThis session explores how to solve this problem by separating aut
 horized access from authorized action. Because any data coming from the AI
  agent can be compromised\, those signals cannot prove the human user is a
 ctually present and consenting. Instead\, high-risk actions require indepe
 ndent\, out-of-band proof of human presence that the AI cannot fake or gen
 erate on its own. This proof is evaluated before the task is executed and 
 generates a legally auditable receipt of the outcome.\nTo solve this\, thi
 s talk introduces HAPS (Human Approval and Presence Specification)\, a dec
 entralized\, open-source design pattern. Available on GitHub under an Apac
 he-2.0 license\, HAPS provides the missing "human intent" boundary for AI-
 driven workflows. It defines how a system can tie verified human approval 
 to a specific action\, rather than mandating a specific method for proving
  human presence. It seamlessly integrates with existing standards like FID
 O passkeys\, WebAuthn\, and OAuth\, and it requires no prior setup by the 
 AI agent or its operator. Specification: github.com/iProov/HAPS\nAttendees
  will leave this session with a practical\, open-source blueprint to secur
 e their systems against unconsented AI agent actions. Specifically\, you w
 ill learn how to:\n\n• Implement the HAPS design pattern using available
  schemas\, test vectors\, and an Apache-2.0 reference implementation.\n•
  Gate your most consequential and high-risk system actions without having 
 to build a solution from scratch or get locked into a single vendor.\n• 
 Incrementally adopt these human-consent safeguards across different AI fra
 meworks to safely enable high-risk use cases like payments and privileged 
 access\, rather than prohibiting them by default.\nAgenda: https://authent
 icatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:bf2d67c51cf795b63b093a1d984eee1cd12e057a@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T162500
SUMMARY:Session To Be Announced
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Agenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026
 /
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fa844e342cbf366972e1735ea5c62a679ef22b20@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T163500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T170000
SUMMARY:Passkeys at eBay Scale: Making Password-less Work Across Platforms\
 , Failure Resilience\, and Global UX Research at Scale
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Manisa Mondal (ebay)\; I
 langovan Vairakkalai (ebay)\nPasskey adoption is no longer the hard part 
 — making passkeys the reliable\, preferred sign-in method at scale is. P
 asskeys have grown to become one of eBay's leading sign-in methods\, with 
 usage increasing steadily over time across platforms.\nCo-presented by a E
 ngineering Lead and Product Manager from eBay's Identity team\, this sessi
 on combines engineering depth with product strategy to share what comes af
 ter the initial passkey rollout.\nWe begin with conditional mediation: how
  eBay uses it as an unconditional trigger for cloud-synced passkeys\, deli
 vering seamless sign-in across new and existing devices. Native apps see t
 he full benefit\; the web has constraints we walk through along with pract
 ical solutions.\nNext\, we cover the full authentication experience. When 
 a passkey-registered user arrives\, eBay auto-triggers the biometric promp
 t. But authentication must never be a dead end — other authentication me
 thods stand ready as instant fallbacks if the passkey prompt fails. We dis
 cuss how to give users control over which method they use without introduc
 ing complexity.\nA significant portion of the session addresses invalid pr
 ivate keys — a growing pain point as passkey usage scales. We present eB
 ay's approach to detecting orphaned keys\, surfacing clear messaging\, and
  providing deletion paths during sign-in and passkey management settings. 
 We also cover strategies for users who experience repeated passkey failure
 s — when to back off and how to guide them forward.\nThese decisions did
 n't happen in a vacuum. A large-scale UX research survey\, studying users 
 who adopted passkeys\, users who enrolled and reverted\, and users who wer
 e unaware - revealed the perception gaps and friction points that directly
  shaped our strategy.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authentic
 ate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:8bd55f3ad08ae8c58ceb80ce163c08661d58bcf1@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T163500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T170000
SUMMARY:Governing Non‑Human Identity for AI Agents in CIAM: Zero Standing
  Privilege in Practice
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Non-human Authentication\nSpeakers: Manikandan Rajaram (
 Capital One)\; Vik Khenat (Capital One)\nYour next privileged user is an a
 gent\, one the customer authorizes to act on their behalf. Consumer apps a
 re rapidly adding agentic copilots that can navigate accounts and complete
  tasks like refunds\, address changes\, plan upgrades\, or money movement.
  That’s a UX win\, and a fraud magnet\; prompt injection\, malicious bro
 wser extensions\, and token replay can turn delegated convenience into acc
 ount takeover and unauthorized transactions across web\, mobile\, and OBO 
 channels.\n\nThis session presents a governance model for customer authori
 zed agents using zero standing privilege (ZSP). Agents hold no persistent 
 account access\; they receive intent scoped\, short lived permissions boun
 d to the runtime (sender constrained / proof‑of‑possession) and to exp
 licit customer consent. We’ll cover patterns for agent registration and 
 attestation\, delegated authorization (OAuth/OIDC)\, step‑up and transac
 tion confirmation for high risk actions\, optional wallet/VC signals for h
 igher assurance\, continuous risk evaluation using behavioral telemetry\, 
 and rapid revocation with audit ready evidence of who authorized what.\n\n
 Attendees will leave with CIAM control objectives\, policy patterns\, and 
 an implementation checklist to enable secure in-app agents and “bring yo
 ur own agent” experiences without sacrificing conversion or support outc
 omes.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Non-human Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:fb097d00c3155a0d4b9376884e3ae7a6a24face2@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T163500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T170000
SUMMARY:Presented by Mastercard: Beyond the Wallet: Trust\, Interoperabilit
 y\, and Identity Verification in the Era of Verifiable Credentials
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Mobile and Digital Identity\nSpeakers: Abuarshad Saifude
 en (Mastercard)\nDigital identity is entering a new phase of global adopti
 on. Initiatives such as the European Digital Identity Wallet (EUDI) under 
 eIDAS 2.0\, India's digital identity ecosystem\, Singapore's Singpass\, an
 d other national identity programs are accelerating the shift from traditi
 onal identity verification toward verifiable credential ecosystems. As gov
 ernments\, financial institutions\, and service providers prepare for a wa
 llet-centric future\, important questions remain: • How should relying p
 arties verify and trust digital credentials issued across different jurisd
 ictions? • What are the emerging best practices for identity proofing\, 
 authentication\, privacy\, and interoperability? • How can organizations
  leverage digital identity wallets to streamline onboarding\, support cros
 s-border KYC\, and reduce friction while maintaining high levels of assura
 nce? This session explores lessons learned from real-world digital identit
 y initiatives and examines the role of relying parties in a rapidly evolvi
 ng trust ecosystem. Attendees will gain practical insights into credential
  verification\, trust frameworks\, interoperability models\, and the growi
 ng convergence toward globally recognized verifiable credentials. The sess
 ion will also discuss how payment networks\, identity providers\, and gove
 rnments can collaborate to establish scalable and interoperable digital id
 entity infrastructure capable of supporting the next generation of trusted
  digital interactions.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenti
 cate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Mobile and Digital Identity
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:05de4de906b188c7ed61d2c6db0f7cfdf855ca74@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T163500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T170000
SUMMARY:Presented by Google: The Future of Account Recovery
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Claire Forszt (Google)\;
  Bill Chen (Google)\nAs sign-in becomes passkey-centric at planet-scale\, 
 the user’s mobile device takes a special position as the “origin” de
 vice of the user’s authentication. It is natural that users’ will some
 times will end up in an account recovery situation where the users will ne
 ed to recover their credential manager\, and thus\, their passkeys\, on to
  a new replacement mobile device. The recovery method has to be very secur
 e and very usable at planet-scale. The advent of digital government IDs gi
 ves us a path to providing such a planet-scale\, robust\, user friendly an
 d secure account recovery option for credential managers on the user’s m
 obile.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:27f96d52d14b02d47cf5f0b82894b8c378d94469@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T171000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T173500
SUMMARY:Advancing Phishing-Resistant Authentication in U.S. Government Depl
 oyments
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Teresa Wu (IDEMIA Public
  Security)\; Joe Scalone (Yubico)\; William Fisher (NCCoE)\nAs U.S. govern
 ment agencies work to strengthening its overall cybersecurity posture\, ph
 ishing resistant authentication is becoming a critical component of modern
  ICAM and Zero Trust architectures. In this presentation track session\, t
 he FIDO Alliance U.S. Government Deployment Special Interest Group (SIG) w
 ill provide a practical\, deployment focused overview of what agencies nee
 d to know to successfully implement FIDO based authentication in alignment
  with NIST SP 800-63-4-B.\nGrounded in the latest edition of the FIDO Alli
 ance white paper\, "Guidance for U.S. Government Agency Deployment of FIDO
  Authentication\," this session examines proven deployment patterns in gov
 ernment environments\, including ICAM alignment and integration with exist
 ing identity platforms. The presentation will emphasize how FIDO complemen
 ts\, rather than replaces\, existing PIV and CAC credentials\, and where i
 t is most suitable—such as for non PIV eligible users\, remote access sc
 enarios\, and specific workforce and application use cases.\nWe will explo
 re practical migration strategies from passwords\, OTP\, and PIV/CAC only 
 models to FIDO enabled architectures.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com
 /event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:13ea0fa8737cddb440c752870fe2de96d6c2280d@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T171000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T173500
SUMMARY:Agentic Commerce: From Scraping to Standards
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Non-human Authentication\nSpeakers: Walmik Deshpande (Pa
 yPal)\nAI shopping agents are here. Amazon Rufus\, ChatGPT\, and Google Ge
 mini are already enabling users to discover and purchase products through 
 conversation. But the infrastructure enabling these experiences is fragmen
 ted: most agents resort to headless browser scraping—a brittle hack that
  breaks on UI changes\, violates Terms of Service\, and doesn't scale.\nTh
 ree protocols emerged in late 2025 to standardize agentic commerce: OpenAI
 's Agentic Commerce Protocol (ACP)\, Google's Universal Commerce Protocol 
 (UCP)\, and Google's Agent Payments Protocol (AP2). Each solves different 
 parts of the problem\, and together they reveal a critical insight: agenti
 c commerce is fundamentally an identity and authorization challenge.\nThis
  talk unpacks the complete technical stack\, with specific focus on how id
 entity primitives enable trust in agent-driven transactions:\nAuthenticati
 on: How passkeys and WebAuthn assertions prove user authorization for auto
 nomous purchases (human-not-present scenarios)\nAuthorization: OAuth 2.0 p
 atterns for agent-to-merchant API access\, refresh token strategies\, and 
 scope design for checkout delegation\nCryptographic Proof: How DIDs (Decen
 tralized Identifiers) and Verifiable Credentials create tamper-evident aud
 it trails—the difference between "the agent claims the user authorized t
 his" and mathematical proof\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/aut
 henticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Non-human Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:e0dbd46c156fa55d7d2e184b2ac876b3829ca436@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T171000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T173500
SUMMARY:Presented by Microsoft: The next identity frontier: Verified Access
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Marina Sanchez (Microsoft)\; Tim Larson (Microsoft)\n
 Passkeys made sign-in phishing-resistant\, but secure authentication alone
  can't confirm who's actually behind the session - and AI is accelerating 
 impersonation and synthetic identity threats across the user lifecycle. In
  this session\, Tim Larson and Marina Sanchez will explore how identity ve
 rification can build on the success of passkeys to protect the moments bef
 ore\, during\, and after authentication. Attendees will learn where identi
 ty confidence can break down and how verified access can help enterprises 
 protect the complete user lifecycle without introducing unnecessary fricti
 on.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:74fb124811330db2aa0db2713fa0cacf4eafd98c@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T171000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261020T173500
SUMMARY:Presented by Yubico: Scale the Strongest Passkeys: Deploy Security 
 Keys Past Privileged Access
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: David Treece (Yubico)\nW
 hen artificial intelligence can compromise identities at scale\, securing 
 only your privileged accounts leaves the rest of your enterprise exposed. 
 True resilience demands extending phishing-resistant authentication to eve
 ry user across the ecosystem. Large enterprises have deployed purpose-buil
 t passkeys\, in the form of hardware security keys\, to all users\, not ju
 st for security but for operational and user experience benefits. Organiza
 tions need practical strategies that align with how they operate from an I
 T\, and business perspective\, such that the full potential of passkeys ca
 n be realized. In this session we will explore creating an integrated pass
 key deployment plan based on real-world examples. The session will also ad
 dress how security keys can be used in the agentic AI landscape to approve
  workflows.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-20
 26/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:5233054325e84f88bcb61b07d2a9bfa8c48715d2@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T083000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T085500
SUMMARY:You Are the Weakest Link\, Goodbye
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Commercial and Regulatory Imperatives\nSpeakers: Heikki 
 Henriksen (Stø AS)\nDigital identity systems are often described as chain
 s — and like any chain\, they fail at their weakest link. After decades 
 of strengthening cryptography\, protocols\, and hardware\, the most persis
 tent vulnerability remains unchanged: the human factor.\n\nThis session dr
 aws on more than two decades of operational experience with BankID in Norw
 ay\, one of the world’s most widely adopted national eID schemes. \n\nWe
  examine the full digital identity lifecycle — from identity proofing an
 d authentication to transaction signing and continuous fraud prevention 
 — and show how attacks rarely succeed by breaking technology\, but by ex
 ploiting people\, processes\, and assumptions around them.\n\nA key theme 
 is the balance between privacy and security. Effective anti‑fraud and ab
 use detection depends on lifecycle visibility and behavioral signals over 
 time\, while privacy requirements demand data minimization and clear purpo
 se limitation. We discuss why these trade‑offs must be made explicit —
  not avoided — to prevent blind spots that benefit attackers rather than
  users.\nThe talk offers practical\, experience‑based insights into iden
 tifying and eliminating the weakest links in modern digital identity ecosy
 stems — before attackers do.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/
 authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Commercial and Regulatory Imperatives
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:02eb456434141aa38c77cdcf559bcd562606b871@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T083000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T085500
SUMMARY:What’s next for passkeys in WebAuthn L4
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth\nSpeake
 rs: Matthew Miller (Cisco Duo)\nThe W3C Web Authentication Working Group (
 WAWG) oversees the other half of FIDO2. With WebAuthn L3 recently finalize
 d\, the Working Group is full steam ahead on L4 discussing and defining th
 e next round of passkey-enabling features for browsers and platforms. Come
  get a rundown of the features being proposed and considered and find out 
 how you can voice your support for the ones that interest you the most.\nA
 genda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:460aa674eb94aec54107f04aa5715ab4cd3b178b@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T083000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T085500
SUMMARY:Account Recovery with FIDO DocAuth Type 5: Your Workforce Can Alrea
 dy Defeat Deepfakes
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Mobile and Digital Identity\nSpeakers: Rob Brown (Signic
 at)\; Paul Tournemaine (Dashlane)\nUn-phishable authentication solves one 
 problem and creates another. When users can no longer be tricked\, attacke
 rs pivot to the next weakest link: the helpdesk. Armed with a headset and 
 a handful of scripted questions\, helpdesk agents are targets for attacker
 s wielding deepfakes\, voice clones\, and intelligence harvested from soci
 al media and data breaches.\n\nHelpdesk agents have an impossible job: hel
 p every legitimate locked-out user regain access quickly while avoiding mo
 re and more attacks. The stakes are mission-critical. Enrolling an attacke
 r's authenticator can bring down the business.\n\nAccount recovery is an i
 dentity verification problem\, and the riskiest verification of all. So wh
 y are we still picking up the phone to do it?\n\nThe FIDO Alliance DocAuth
  2.0 specification gives every organization a defense that has been hiding
  in plain sight. Type 5 documents carry a cryptographic secret: like a FID
 O security key\, they respond to a challenge\, prove their authenticity on
  the spot\, and\, critically\, deepfakes don't have chips in their fake ID
 s.\n\nIn this session\, we'll show how two organizations strengthened thei
 r account recovery posture and turned their workforces into the solution. 
 We'll walk through how data privacy impact assessments and security concer
 ns were addressed\, how training was delivered\, and how both projects rea
 ched full rollout in weeks.\n\nThe help desk no longer needs to do the imp
 ossible. \nAnd a critical\, overlooked business process can finally be de-
 risked.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Mobile and Digital Identity
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:3fadc95450bddc42060d304b6562c570ecacaa5a@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T083000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T085500
SUMMARY:Passkeys and Authentication at the Frontiers of XR\, Wearables\, an
 d AI-first Devices
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication\nSpeakers: 
 Paul Wright (Meta)\nAs the broader industry embraces passkeys having close
 d many technical gaps and usability issues for ubiquitous and familiar dev
 ices\, there is a looming new frontier of authentication demanding passwor
 dless and phishing-resistant standards. This session will detail Meta's ex
 perience tackling uncharted territory\, unique challenges\, and our approa
 ch to these problems.\n\nWe will present an overview on what makes this cl
 ass of devices unique–such as display and input–and where existing par
 adigms are subtly different or fail entirely. We will share our experience
  building on AOSP without Play Services\, lack of off-the-shelf cross-plat
 form solutions\, and the relationships between service providers and conte
 nt providers we have navigated. Lastly we will talk about why this matters
  to users\, centering their experience and our ambitions to build a more h
 uman-centric UX.\n\nAttendees will leave with the knowledge of how Meta ha
 s navigated these challenges\, a practical understanding of how to extend 
 passkey authentication to this class of devices and new paradigms\, and a 
 better understanding of how authentication is evolving in this new frontie
 r.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:de3000685b2058366bbdd6b474f94e1c51600875@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T090500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T093000
SUMMARY:Hardening EUDI wallets for Strong Customer Authentication under PSR
 /PSD3
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Commercial and Regulatory Imperatives\nSpeakers: Frederi
 k Mennes (OneSpan)\; Jeremy Grant (Venable LLP)\nThe mandate to support Eu
 ropean digital identity (EUDI) wallets for Strong Customer Authentication 
 (SCA)\, together with the shift from PSD2 to PSR/PSD3\, presents European 
 banks with various technical and compliance challenges.\n\nThis session pr
 ovides an overview of technical and compliance challenges involved in the 
 deployment of EUDI wallets\, and outlines how banks can overcome them. We 
 will focus on the following topics:\n* Outlining the current regulatory re
 quirements: we will focus on SCA requirements in the digital identity and 
 payment services regulations\, and highlight areas where further clarifica
 tion is required.\n* Hardening the possession factor: under SCA\, the “p
 ossession” factor must be unique to the user. We will discuss possible t
 echnical approaches for device-bound SCA.\n* Collecting risk signals for f
 raud detection: the PSR emphasizes the need for banks to include device an
 d behavioural intelligence into their fraud monitoring systems. We will ex
 plore how banks can extract and ingest telemetry from EUDI wallets.\n* Dyn
 amic linking with wallets: a key requirement of PSD2 and PSR consists of d
 ynamic linking. We will demonstrate how to wrap EUDI wallet presentations 
 in a secure request/response flow that cryptographically binds the authent
 ication to a specific payee and amount.\n\nAttendees will obtain a clear u
 nderstanding of how banks can leverage EUDI wallets as a robust authentica
 tion mechanism\, meeting compliance requirements\, reducing fraud liabilit
 y and streamlining the customer journey.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.
 com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Commercial and Regulatory Imperatives
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:798fd62f121104a27bf62ad449320989a578cd4a@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T090500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T093000
SUMMARY:Delegated Authorization in 2026: An Insider’s Roadmap
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth\nSpeake
 rs: Jen Schreiber (Crowdstrike)\nDelegated authorization is having a momen
 t – and not a quiet one. It’s showing up in the messy\, human edges of
  our systems: a parent managing a child’s identity\, a partner navigatin
 g their loved one’s digital access after death\, and the sudden anxiety 
 of an AI agent granted just enough autonomy to make a life-altering financ
 ial error.\n\nNew models and drafts are emerging rapidly within the OpenID
  Foundation\, IETF\, and Kantara Initiative to tackle these complexities. 
 But as these solutions evolve\, we must ask: can they truly support the nu
 anced trust relationships now required in our IAM systems?\n\nThis talk pr
 ovides an insider’s roadmap to the current delegated authorization lands
 cape\, helping practitioners navigate emerging specs and understand the in
 tended impact of each. Drawing upon direct conversations with standards au
 thors\, primary use cases\, and proposed solutions\, we will highlight the
  central themes of these efforts and the critical gaps that none yet addre
 ss.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:30677bf38f0cc25e1e6671ddc7fc6adad498c308@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T090500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T093000
SUMMARY:Credential Security Properties: Progress Report
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication\nSpeakers: 
 Pamela Dingle (Microsoft)\nTwo years ago\, Pamela Dingle & Dean H. Saxe ar
 gued that the identity industry needed to stop counting authentication fac
 tors and start reasoning about the properties of the authentication event.
  The response from the community was strong. Since then\, their work has m
 oved from conference stage to standards body resulting in an IETF draft fo
 rmalizing terminology with a goal to develop a precise\, interoperable voc
 abulary for describing what actually happened during authentication events
 . In this talk\, Pamela will walk through the terminology the draft define
 s: what the terms mean\, why the distinctions matter\, and how identity pr
 oviders and relying parties can use them today to drive clearer authentica
 tion and authorization decisions. Standardization of credential security p
 roperties requires the identity community's input. If you have deployed pa
 sskeys\, built policy engines on top of authentication context\, or strugg
 led to describe authentication events consistently across federated system
 s\, your experience is directly relevant to this work.\nAgenda: https://au
 thenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:abce3fa6754518c0caaf1aaaae5023bbf47b2c40@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T094000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T103000
SUMMARY:EU Cyber Resilience Act: Secured-by-Design Imperatives for Passkeys
 \, Identity Systems & Access Control
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Commercial and Regulatory Imperatives\nSpeakers: PreetiO
 hri Khemani (Infineon)\nThe EU Cyber Resilience Act (CRA) fundamentally re
 shapes how products with digital elements\, hardware and software\, are de
 signed\, documented\, tested\, delivered\, and maintained for the EU marke
 t. Identity and logical access control components are squarely in scope. T
 hat includes authenticators and passkeys\, credential managers\, identity 
 platforms\, privileged access tools\, SDKs/libraries\, firmware for authen
 ticators and secure elements\, and the remote services that underpin them.
 \n\nThis masterclass translates CRA obligations into concrete actions for 
 identity leaders deploying or building passkeys and modern access controls
 . We’ll map CRA’s essential cybersecurity requirements\, secured by de
 fault configurations\, risk-appropriate cryptography\, update and support 
 lifecycles\, coordinated vulnerability disclosure and incident reporting\,
  technical documentation and component inventories (SBOM-like)\, post‑ma
 rket monitoring\, and conformity assessment\, onto the identity stack. We
 ’ll cover how CRA’s product categories can impact conformity assessmen
 t routes for IAM and authentication products\, and how to leverage harmoni
 zed standards\, assurance schemes\, and FIDO certifications as evidence (w
 ithout assuming they are sufficient on their own).\n\nAttendees will leave
  with a practical roadmap\, patterns to reduce friction\, and an implement
 able playbook that you can adapt and use immediately. Whether you build or
  buy\, this session will help you align passkey and authentication program
 s with regulatory expectations while accelerating phishing-resistant authe
 ntication for Humans and Agents.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/even
 t/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Commercial and Regulatory Imperatives
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:d78b3e5c26cd9b7b43fb79650291904b0566b492@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T094000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T103000
SUMMARY:Agentic AI\, Identity\, and Who Is on the Hook When Things Go Wrong
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Non-human Authentication\nSpeakers: Frances Zelazny (Pro
 ve)\; Steve Smith (Consult Hyperion)\; Josh Tompkins (Charles Schwab)\; Ju
 die Rinearson (K&L Gates)\nAs AI agents begin to initiate logins\, move mo
 ney\, transact and change account settings on behalf of users\, a fundamen
 tal question emerges: who is actually acting\, and who is responsible when
  it goes wrong? This session brings together leaders from financial servic
 es\, legal\, and digital identity industries to examine how agentic AI res
 hapes identity\, authentication\, authorization and liability.\n\nSpeakers
  will explore scenarios where AI agents access sensitive data and accounts
 \, trigger payments\, or interact with support\, and how passkeys\, and st
 rong identity proofing can be used to bind agents to a verified human. We 
 will explore how continuous device\, phone\, and behavioral signals from p
 roviders can detect anomalous agent behavior in real time\, without negati
 vely impacting user experience.\n\nSpeakers will share their internal adop
 ter’s view: how they are rolling out AI tools and early agents inside a 
 highly confidential environment\, the guardrails they’ve put in place\, 
 and the kinds of reasonable controls that are under consideration when sof
 tware acts on behalf of employees and customers. Building on those lessons
 \, the panel will dive into allocation of risk between financial instituti
 ons\, AI providers\, and identity providers\, how kill switches and overri
 de flows might be leveraged\, and how to explain these choices to regulato
 rs\, auditors\, and customers. Attendees will leave with practical pattern
 s for safely enabling agentic AI atop FIDO based authentication\, and a cl
 earer view of who should own which risks along the way.\nAgenda: https://a
 uthenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Non-human Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:dae0aba0aabfb49f36299faf42a4a3288e11fb63@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T094000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T103000
SUMMARY:Biometrics and Identity Verification in a World of Deepfakes – A 
 Practical Guide
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Mobile and Digital Identity\nSpeakers: Chris Allgrove (I
 ngenium Biometric Laboratories)\; Stephanie Schuckers (University of North
  Carolina - Charlotte)\nBiometric recognition is a critical step in identi
 ty verification\, authentication\, and account recovery. Liveness detectio
 n protects systems against attackers which may target individuals by autom
 atically determining if it is real\, live person in front of the camera or
  a printed image of a face or other form of presentation attack. With the 
 rapid advancement of artificial intelligence\, deepfake generation\, like 
 face swapping\, is easier than ever and can target individuals to make a f
 ace appear real. These face swaps can be used to bypass the camera\, somet
 imes called injection attacks. There are both cybersecurity protections as
  well as liveness detection that can detect and prevent these types of att
 acks. This session will cover the basics of presentation attacks\, injecti
 on attacks\, and methods of detection as well as show specific examples. T
 his session will also cover FIDO Biometric Certification programs which ar
 e based on ISO standards with requirements developed by industry subject m
 atter experts and evaluated by an international network of FIDO accredited
  laboratories.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s
 -2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Mobile and Digital Identity
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:187d9446e8d8f86a1ca34ee03998bacb165dd7fd@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T094000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T103000
SUMMARY:Presented by Microsoft: Beyond Passwords: Building a Phishing-Resis
 tant Enterprise with Passkeys
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Masterclass\nSpeakers: Sangeeta Ranjit (Microsoft)\; Han
 a Kim (Microsoft)\nOrganizations have spent years deploying MFA\, yet acco
 unt compromise remains one of the top causes of security incidents. Attack
 ers continue to bypass traditional protections through phishing\, social e
 ngineering\, MFA fatigue\, token theft\, and credential interception attac
 ks. The reality is simple: as long as organizations rely on phishable cred
 entials\, users remain vulnerable and protecting authentication only is no
 t sufficient anymore.This session shows how to move beyond MFA to prevent 
 phishing and impersonation attacks.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/e
 vent/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Masterclass
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:be03d4f8d6bef81b6e7acd5dd107f87c4a0e9324@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T112500
SUMMARY:Disabling Passwords on a Consumer Platform with Tens of Millions of
  Users
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Tatsuya Karino (Mercari\
 , inc.)\nAt Authenticate 2025\, Mercari presented a three-phase plan to el
 iminate phishing: improve phishing-resistant account’s UX\, grow passkey
  adoption\, and eventually eliminate passwords. At that time\, most of the
  plan was still a roadmap. One year later\, we are ready to share the resu
 lts.\n\nWe completed the phishing-resistant account’s UX improvements th
 at were prerequisites for password deprecation: risk-based alternative log
 in using magic link with proximity restrictions\, and expanded account rec
 overy through Japan's Digital Identity Verification App\, third-party iden
 tity proofing vendors\, and Digital Credentials API.\n\nWe then began phas
 ed password deprecation: removing passwords from new account registration\
 , risk-based password blocking triggered by user behavior signals and logi
 n-time risk detection. In parallel\, we accelerated passkey growth through
  point reward campaigns and automatic passkey upgrades.\n\nThis presentati
 on shows the data: declining password usage\, growing passkey adoption\, a
 nd a projected timeline for full password elimination. A practical case st
 udy from a consumer platform with tens of millions of users\, actively mak
 ing passwords obsolete.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authent
 icate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:294d575230619e09e63c691fcbef79df71584e53@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T112500
SUMMARY:IAM for AI: From "Eh\, I?" to "I am."
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Non-human Authentication\nSpeakers: Brian Campbell (Ping
  Identity)\nComparable only to seismic shifts like the Industrial Revoluti
 on or the rise of the Internet\, the emergence of artificial intelligence 
 is unquestionably transformative. But does it really demand an equally rad
 ical upheaval in IAM standards and technology? With the seemingly endless 
 parade of prophets and profiteers declaring the revolution imminent and as
 suring us that they alone can lead it\, you might think so.\nThe reality m
 ight be less dramatic and more practical. Many of the core challenges of '
 IAM for AI' look pretty familiar. Regular old OAuth is already well suited
  to a surprising amount of AI systems' needs\, and emerging efforts like O
 Auth Client ID Metadata Document (CIMD) and Cross-app Access seem poised t
 o fill in some of the gaps. It may be that our existing IAM foundation can
  rise to the occasion\, or that our expectations can be tempered to meet t
 he moment.\nJoin a curmudgeonly pragmatist and occasional identity industr
 y contributor on a journey from 'Eh\, I?' to 'I am.' without inciting revo
 lution.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Non-human Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:af738a2518789a319ddeb08b9632b9305d403053@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T112500
SUMMARY:Scaling Digital Credentials UX for Agentic Commerce
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Mobile and Digital Identity\nSpeakers: Jean-Pierre Prall
 e (Google)\nThis presentation explores the foundational UX strategy for sc
 aling digital credentials\, positioning them as the essential building blo
 ck for the next era of Agentic Commerce. As we transition from manual auth
 entication to intelligence-driven\, automated verification\, we propose a 
 unified framework that positions digital credentials not as mere "copies" 
 of physical IDs\, but as sovereign\, cryptographic assets. By establishing
  a secure identity and payment layer at the OS level\, we aim to deliver a
  frictionless\, "single-tap" experience that harmonizes Payments\, Authent
 ication\, and Identity.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authent
 icate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Mobile and Digital Identity
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:417cf34833d17e5fb4c6989356ab6b3f17238eb3@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T112500
SUMMARY:Authenticating Authority in the Age of Agentic AI
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication\nSpeakers: 
 Abbie Barbir (CVS Health and Secure Identity Alliance (SIA))\; Clayton Rot
 h (Private Identity)\nPasskeys significantly improve phishing-resistant au
 thentication by using relying-party-bound public-key credentials secured o
 n user devices. However\, they assume a direct human-to-service interactio
 n—an assumption increasingly challenged by the rise of autonomous AI age
 nts and delegated access to caregivers\, colleagues\, and other representa
 tives.\nAs AI agents begin executing transactions\, accessing sensitive da
 ta\, and operating asynchronously\, relying parties must move beyond simpl
 e credential validation. They need to determine who authorized an action\,
  what authority was delegated\, and under what constraints. In regulated s
 ectors like healthcare\, finance\, and government\, this attribution is cr
 itical for accountability\, compliance\, and non-repudiation.\nThis work e
 xplores emerging identity architectures\, including efforts within the Sec
 ure Identity Alliance (SIA)\, that address these challenges. The approach 
 combines high-assurance identity proofing (NIST SP 800-63 IAL2/AAL2)\, pas
 skeys and FIDO authenticators\, on-device biometrics\, and contextual trus
 t signals (device integrity\, geolocation\, behavior\, and logs) to establ
 ish a strong identity root.\nFrom this foundation\, users can issue crypto
 graphically verifiable credentials and delegate authority to AI agents or 
 other individuals with explicit constraints on scope\, duration\, and cont
 ext—supporting “human-in-the-loop” requirements.\nThe discussion exa
 mines how agentic AI disrupts traditional authentication models\, outlines
  delegation and authorization flows anchored in passkeys\, and analyzes ke
 y risks such as credential sharing\, agent impersonation\, privilege escal
 ation\, and uncontrolled automation. It also highlights when re-authorizat
 ion is required for sensitive act\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/eve
 nt/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ac3abfe22e5b757c25688ac544b5d43c0fba9da6@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T113500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T120000
SUMMARY:The Frontline Authentication Gap: Balancing Fast Access with Strong
  Security
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Ronnie Manning (FIDO All
 iance)\; PJ Boone (Imprivata)\nFrontline authentication is an operational 
 issue\, not simply a security control. New research from the FIDO Alliance
  and Imprivata among 700 authentication and identity decision-makers acros
 s the US\, UK\, France and Germany finds that 83% of organizations use sha
 red endpoints\, while only 38% say nearly all frontline workers use unique
  credentials. Authentication issues affect at least one-quarter of frontli
 ne workers each month in 56% of organizations\, with affected workers inte
 rrupted around 11 times monthly and losing approximately 14 minutes per in
 cident.\nThe consequences extend beyond lost productivity. Ninety-five per
 cent of organizations report authentication-driven workarounds\, including
  staying logged in\, bypassing controls and sharing credentials. At the sa
 me time\, 62% report confirmed or suspected unauthorized recovery\, reset 
 or unlock attempts.\nThis session will explore how organizations can desig
 n authentication around real frontline workflows\, including shared device
 s\, rapid user switching\, legacy systems and account recovery. It will ex
 amine where passwordless methods are delivering value\, why adoption remai
 ns transitional\, and how stronger identity assurance can improve both ope
 rational continuity and security. Attend this session to:\n\n• Identify 
 the authentication challenges created by shared devices and frontline work
 flows.\n• Assess the operational and security impact of authentication f
 riction.\n• Evaluate the practical benefits and adoption barriers associ
 ated with passwordless authentication.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.co
 m/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2f712a508782076052cdb9ddd0c61854102bc66b@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T113500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T120000
SUMMARY:API Keys are not for AI Agents
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Non-human Authentication\nSpeakers: Swarna Kavi (Capital
  One)\; Anoop Gopi (Capital One)\nAutonomous agents are already creating t
 ickets\, running playbooks\, and invoking cloud and SaaS APIs across multi
 ple teams and tenants. One leaked key\, copied secret\, or prompt‑inject
 ed tool call can turn “helpful automation” into high blast radius chan
 ge\, while credential sprawl makes it hard to answer questions such as\, w
 hich agent did what\, under whose authority\, and can we revoke it now?\n\
 nThis session shows an agent‑first access pattern that replaces standing
  secrets with intent scoped\, just in time authorization. We’ll walk thr
 ough reference flows for onboarding and identity attestation\, issuing sho
 rt‑lived permissions bound to the executing runtime (sender constrained 
 / proof‑of‑possession)\, and granting access only for the declared int
 ent or task. We’ll also cover continuous access evaluation using runtime
  telemetry and shared risk signals\, plus practical patterns for delegatio
 n\, revocation\, and audit when agents can call tools and trigger side eff
 ects.\n\nAttendees will leave with an architecture blueprint\, a threat mo
 del\, and an implementation checklist mapped to open standards (OAuth/OIDC
 \, and Verifiable Credentials) for multi‑cloud and cross‑domain adopti
 on.\nTake Away Bullets\n\nKill agent API keys: use intent scoped JIT acces
 s. \nContinuous auth: use signals\, revoke fast\, prove who did what.\nBin
 d tokens to runtime (PoP)\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authe
 nticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Non-human Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:c2527fcb5fb2c80fc31443241f8fc1422cb35ca4@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T113500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T120000
SUMMARY:Seamless Identity Migration—The Next Frontier
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Mobile and Digital Identity\nSpeakers: Rolf Lindemann (O
 neSpan)\nPasskeys have set a high bar for seamless device migration—user
 s expect to switch devices without losing access or compromising security.
  But as identity credentials move into mobile wallets at scale\, the migra
 tion story becomes far more complex. Wallets aren’t just about authentic
 ation\; they carry payment instruments\, identity proofs\, and sensitive a
 ttestations. Unlike passkeys\, wallet migration must address regulatory co
 nstraints\, cryptographic binding\, and user trust in a way that feels eff
 ortless yet secure.\n\nThis session explores why the migration problem for
  wallets is harder than for passkeys and what’s at stake if we fail to s
 olve it. We’ll examine the technical challenges—secure transfer of cre
 dentials\, maintaining provenance\, and preventing cloning or replay attac
 ks—alongside usability hurdles like recovery flows and cross-platform po
 rtability. We’ll also look at emerging standards and architectural patte
 rns that could enable secure migration without breaking the user experienc
 e.\n\nParticipants will leave with a clear understanding of:\n•Why walle
 t migration is a critical enabler for adoption of identity wallets.\n•Th
 e gaps in current approaches compared to passkey migration.\n•Practical 
 strategies and design principles for secure\, user-friendly migration.\n\n
 If passkeys made migration feel magical\, wallets demand a new level of in
 novation. Join us to learn how to make “switching devices” as seamless
 —and trustworthy—as users expect in a world where your phone is your p
 assport\, your bank\, and your identity vault.\nAgenda: https://authentica
 tecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Mobile and Digital Identity
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:781f2d56b8e29b351f4c149890601e867c04cdb3@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T113500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T120000
SUMMARY:Context-Bound Identity: Enforcing Authority Boundaries When AI Agen
 ts Act on Behalf of Humans
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication\nSpeakers: 
 Kaz Miyata (Rakuten Group Inc.)\nPasskeys have transformed how humans prov
 e who they are. A harder question is emerging: when an AI agent acts on yo
 ur behalf—booking\, buying\, transferring\, deciding—how does the syst
 em know it is allowed to act\, in which domain\, and who is accountable wh
 en something goes wrong?\n\nMost identity systems were built for humans lo
 gging in\, not for agents operating across banking\, commerce\, and financ
 ial services with different rules\, regulators\, and trust levels. This se
 ssion introduces Context-Bound Identity (CBI)\, a practical architectural 
 approach discussed at Rakuten Group\, a large consumer ecosystem serving 1
 00M+ members across shopping\, banking\, securities\, and mobile services.
  CBI is built on a simple principle: AI agents can act for you\, but they 
 can never be you—humans stay in control.\n\nCBI applies three design rul
 es: (1) agents may access the information they need across services\, but 
 cannot take actions outside explicitly granted\, domain-scoped authority\;
  (2) high-risk actions are attributable to a real human decision\, confirm
 ed with a passkey (step-up / transaction approval) and recorded for audit\
 ; (3) the system composes authorization fail-closed across domains—if an
 y required service rejects the request\, the overall workflow stops.\n\nWe
 ’ll walk through a real-world scenario: a high-value purchase where an a
 gent must coordinate across a shopping service\, a bank account\, and a se
 curities portfolio—safely and accountably\, without giving the agent a m
 aster key. Attendees will leave with a clear view of where today’s ident
 ity systems break for agents\, a practical framework for human-in-the-loop
  control at ecosystem scale\, and concrete first steps to apply in their o
 wn organization.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u
 -s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:c3f6ce5a445e171d2a63915889c981378f9a0457@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T121000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T123500
SUMMARY:No Passwords\, No Google\, No Problem: Going Passkey-First for NGOs
  and Journalists
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Michel Weksler (mothertr
 ee labs)\nMost passkey talks come from big tech companies with millions of
  users. This one comes from a small team building a collaboration platform
  for NGOs\, journalists\, and legal organizations — groups where a phish
 ing attack isn't an inconvenience\, it's a safety risk.\n\nWe started with
  "Sign in with Google" because it was easy. But for organizations that nee
 d to control their own identity\, outsourcing authentication to Google was
  a contradiction. So we went passkey-first using Keycloak's built-in WebAu
 thn support — and were surprised by how ready the device landscape actua
 lly was.\n\nThen reality intervened. A new team member in Europe had no pa
 sskey-capable device. Rather than reintroduce passwords\, we added magic l
 ink authentication as a fallback — keeping the entire flow passwordless 
 and phishing-resistant.\n\nThis talk is the story of each transition: from
  OAuth to passkeys to magic links\, what drove each decision\, and what we
  got wrong along the way. You'll hear real numbers on device support and a
 doption\, and leave with a practical playbook for going passkey-first — 
 even when you can't guarantee every user has the hardware.\nAgenda: https:
 //authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:2eec9b0273211a7ad5a4d2dd0bc2b44039bb59e7@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T121000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T123500
SUMMARY:Trustworthy AI Agents with Verifiable Credentials: Identity\, Deleg
 ation\, Authorization
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Non-human Authentication\nSpeakers: Darrell Geusz (Ping 
 Identity)\nAs AI agents move from demos to production and become more auto
 nomous and interact with one another\, trust collapses without verifiable 
 identity\, controlled delegation\, and tamper-evident intent. This session
  discusses using W3C Verifiable Credentials (VCs) and decentralized identi
 fiers (DIDs) to make agent interactions trustworthy across three dimension
 s:\n\nAgent identity\, provenance and context: Bind an agent instance to a
  cryptographically verifiable issuer and runtime\, so humans and services 
 can confirm the source and identity of the agent in A2Human\, A2A\, and A2
 Service flows. We’ll cover issuance models\, rotating instance keys\, an
 d verifiable presentations (e.g.\, OIDC4VC / Presentation Exchange) so eve
 ry call includes portable proof of “who” the agent is.\nDelegation wit
 h least privilege: Grant time-boxed\, scope-limited access from a human to
  an agent using capability credentials and constrained tokens (audience\, 
 resource\, purpose\, expiry). We’ll map this to real systems—consent U
 X\, policy attachments\, and revocation/update patterns—so agents can ac
 t without overreach.\nIrrefutable authorizations: Capture the mission spec
 ification as a signed\, hashed artifact (including inputs\, constraints\, 
 and expected outputs) to produce cryptographic receipts and audit trails. 
 This enables stakeholders to verify what was asked\, who asked it\, and wh
 at the agent executed—without leaking sensitive content.\nAgenda: https:
 //authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Non-human Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:575f1b3b4eefeb00e302682af2ccbe831269c957@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T121000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T123500
SUMMARY:Deploying identity wallets with passkeys  - Lessons from pilots acr
 oss three continents
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Mobile and Digital Identity\nSpeakers: Leif Johansson (S
 IROS Foundation)\; Stina Ehrensvärd (SIROS Foundation)\nDigital identity 
 wallets are moving quickly from concept to deployment. Governments and org
 anizations are no longer asking if they should adopt wallets\; they are as
 king how to make them work at scale.\n\nThis session draws on SIROS pilot 
 deployments across Europe\, Asia\, and North America\, including collabora
 tions with government agencies in Germany\, Sweden\, Singapore\, Canada\, 
 and France\, as well as a global network of journalists operating across j
 urisdictions.\n\nA consistent thread across these pilots has been the use 
 of passkeys as the primary authentication mechanism. While passkeys promis
 e phishing-resistant\, user-friendly authentication\, their behavior in wa
 llet-based systems introduces new design considerations\, especially when 
 used across devices\, platforms\, and trust domains.\n\nThe session will w
 alk through concrete implementation lessons\, including how recovery mecha
 nisms based on pseudonymous identifiers were tested to avoid long-term cor
 relation\, and how integration with supporting infrastructure (such as Lon
 gfellow and Vega zero-knowledge proof projects for cross-system trust coor
 dination) shaped deployment decisions.\n\nRather than presenting a single 
 architecture\, this talk focuses on patterns we are observing across multi
 ple environments: what is working\, what is failing\, and what remains unr
 esolved.\n\nThe intended audience is architects\, engineers\, and product 
 leaders responsible for deploying or evaluating wallet-based identity syst
 ems who need to understand the tradeoffs that only become visible in real-
 world use.\nThis is a session about deployment reality. It is not a produc
 t demonstration\, nor is it a theoretical overview.\nAgenda: https://authe
 nticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Mobile and Digital Identity
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:7d31183644008eda0d8b9ba600f8c49456265316@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T121000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T123500
SUMMARY:Real-time Authorization as Infrastructure: Preparing for an Agentic
  Future
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication\nSpeakers: 
 Ryan Allen (General Motors)\; Jeremy Erickson (General Motors)\nAuthorizat
 ion has quietly become one of the least scalable parts of modern systems. 
 As enforcement logic is repeatedly re‑implemented across services and co
 debases\, access control embedded in application code becomes brittle\, in
 consistent\, and difficult to govern.\nGM’s AuthZ 2.0 project reframes a
 uthorization enforcement as an infrastructure concern\, not an application
  responsibility. Instead of embedding permission logic into every service\
 , access is enforced uniformly at the infrastructure layer\, with policies
  defined once\, centrally\, using simple\, human‑understandable concepts
 . This model is designed to scale horizontally to support billions of annu
 al authorization decisions.\nService owners define who can access their re
 sources and under what conditions. Mandatory access control policies can b
 e overlaid using standardized signals delivered through the Shared Signals
  Framework (SSF)\, including CAEP events\, allowing enforcement to update 
 in real time. This layered model allows security teams to introduce global
  guardrails without disrupting service ownership.\nAuthZ 2.0 supports defi
 ning policies that govern arbitrary\, application‑level resources—APIs
 \, objects\, actions—while enforcing those policies at the network bound
 ary rather than inside business logic. Applications describe resources\; i
 nfrastructure decides whether access is allowed.\nCentralized\, structured
  policy enables new workflows as systems become more automated. Policies c
 an be queried and analyzed by AI\, enabling agents to reason about access\
 , detect unsafe configurations\, and explain enforcement decisions. Combin
 ed with CI/CD\, AI can evolve access policy alongside code changes and dep
 loyments\, updating access policies automatically as systems change.\nAgen
 da: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cebc8c3bd4776c605bb1f9a3ba228ae83124f828@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T123500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T133500
SUMMARY:Luncheon: Architects of Agentic AI: Building the Systems and the Ca
 reers Behind Them
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Speakers: Tina Srivastava (IDPro)\; Michele Leroux Bustamante (
 IDPro)\nAgentic AI is moving from demo to deployment\, and identity profes
 sionals are discovering they sit squarely in its path. Every autonomous ag
 ent that acts on a user's behalf must be authenticated\, authorized\, scop
 ed\, delegated to\, and held accountable — problems the identity communi
 ty has spent decades solving for humans and machines\, now arriving all at
  once and at machine speed. This session looks at agentic AI from the prac
 titioner's seat: what changes in how we design authentication\, authorizat
 ion\, and governance when the actor is an agent rather than a person\; whe
 re existing patterns like delegation\, least privilege\, non-human identit
 y\, and audit still hold and where they break\; and what all of this means
  for the identity career. Rather than another tour of what agents can do\,
  we'll focus on the work itself: the architecture decisions\, the hard tra
 de-offs\, and the skills and credibility identity practitioners need to le
 ad this shift rather than react to it.\nHosted by IDPro\nAgenda: https://a
 uthenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:c7bcc3fa965f2ae4ef95d71ed5a38bbb7ec67f3c@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T142500
SUMMARY:From Passwords to Passkeys: Customer Learnings from 465M Amazon Cus
 tomers
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Ben Christ (Amazon)\; Sa
 nyal Chincholikar (Amazon)\nPasskeys are often discussed as a technology s
 hift. At Amazon\, we learned they are fundamentally a customer experience 
 shift. Today\, more than 465 million Amazon customers have enrolled passke
 ys\, giving us a unique opportunity to understand how real customers inter
 act with passwordless authentication. This session will share practical le
 arnings from scaling passkeys at internet scale - tailwinds\, headwinds\, 
 where customers experienced friction\, and why many still choose passwords
  when available. \n\nThe session will also look ahead at the future of dig
 ital identity beyond passkeys. As Digital IDs\, wallet-based identity\, an
 d AI agents become more mainstream\, identity systems will increasingly ne
 ed to authenticate not only users\, but also trusted devices\, wallets\, a
 nd agents acting on behalf of customers with explicit consent and strong c
 ryptographic trust. This talk is not a protocol deep dive\; it is a custom
 er-focused discussion on how authentication is evolving toward more seamle
 ss\, trustworthy\, and largely invisible experiences for customers.\nAgend
 a: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Passkeys in Practice
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:3f2b786a07311aa01c55948f3141fdead28d8658@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T142500
SUMMARY:Agentic commerce and Verifiable intent: securing the next generatio
 n of payments
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Non-human Authentication\nSpeakers: Jonathan Grossar (Ma
 stercard)\; Fahad Saleem (Mastercard)\nAs commerce evolves toward agent-dr
 iven interactions\, where digital agents act on behalf of users to execute
  transactions\, traditional models of authentication and authorization are
  no longer sufficient. Ensuring that payments are both secure and aligned 
 with user intent has become a critical challenge. This session explores th
 e role of verifiable intent as a foundational mechanism to protect agentic
  payment transactions – it presents key use cases to illustrate how veri
 fiable intent can limit disputes and enhance trust between consumers\, age
 nts and merchants. It will also cover best practices for implementation\, 
 observed benefits and remaining challenges. Finally\, it will outline the 
 next steps to scale adoption including standardization efforts and ecosyst
 em collaboration.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-
 u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Non-human Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:d2710320c30a0f79280d2b3161ab39f20714e152@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T142500
SUMMARY:Hacking Passkeys: Implementation Weaknesses in the Wild
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth\nSpeake
 rs: Geoff Robinson (ivision)\nWebAuthn and passkeys continue to become mor
 e commonplace. Passkeys promise to be "more secure" by avoiding the pitfal
 ls of passwords\, but the huge variety of emerging implementations are rel
 atively untested. And we know that even if the protocol is bulletproof\, a
  given deployment can be anything but. With an offensive application secur
 ity lens\, what tricks can we use to spoof authentication or elevate privi
 leges?\n\nIn this talk\, Geoff will review his efforts to identify vulnera
 bilities and patterns of weaknesses in deployed WebAuthn and passkey-based
  authentication systems. Geoff has worked over the last two years to threa
 t model passkeys\, systematically document passkey deployments in the wild
 \, and fingerprint their underlying implementations. Geoff got an early st
 art testing and securing real world passkey deployments\, beginning with a
  large-scale deployment for a client in 2021.\nAgenda: https://authenticat
 econ.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:279a86dd00309105bde16a8ccb1507bc0845b3ca@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T142500
SUMMARY:Beyond Login: Securing Sessions in a Post-Password World
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication\nSpeakers: 
 Brandon Traffanstedt (Palo Alto Networks)\nWe’ve spent years trying to e
 liminate the password\, and with FIDO and passkeys\, we are finally winnin
 g. But in real-world deployments\, a familiar pattern has emerged: attacke
 rs have stopped trying to guess passwords and started targeting long-lived
  sessions instead. \n\nEven with MFA\, "Adversary-in-the-Middle" attacks c
 an steal session tokens. These "digital valet keys" bypass all security\, 
 granting attackers immediate access without requiring further passwords or
  biometrics. \n\nThis session explores how practitioners can extend FIDO g
 rade assurance beyond the login event and into the session lifecycle itsel
 f. Drawing from real-world incident patterns\, we’ll examine practical\,
  deployable approaches including hardware-bound sessions\, device and cont
 ext attestation\, step up reassertion of phishing-resistant authentication
  for sensitive actions\, and continuous risk evaluation across human users
 \, machine workloads\, and emerging agentic AI identities.\n\nBrandon Traf
 fanstedt will share frontline evidence of how attackers exploit these pers
 istent sessions to move laterally across human users\, machine workloads\,
  and agentic AI identities. Attendees will walk away with a decision frame
 work for when continuous assurance meaningfully reduces risk – and when 
 it simply adds friction – based on session lifetime\, privilege level\, 
 and attacker tradecraft.\n\nAttendees will take away:\n- A step-by-step gu
 ide to moving from "one-time" security to "continuous" security.\n- A clea
 r look at how modem token theft works so you can explain the risk to your 
 leadership\n- Three specific ways to configure your existing tools to "bin
 d" sessions to hardware.\n- How to introduce FIDO-based re-assertion for h
 igh-risk actions\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u
 -s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:acb26f08ea87cf17433296555f2a7f5f3c2aa1d9@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T143500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T150000
SUMMARY:Passkeys on WhatsApp: Sign in with Passkeys and Beyond
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Passkeys in Practice\nSpeakers: Mayank Manuja (Meta)\; A
 shish Choudhary (Meta)\nWhatsApp first launched passkeys as a way to log i
 n more securely and easily. Since then\, we’ve expanded how passkeys can
  be used: from adding end-to-end encryption to chat backups\, to authorizi
 ng payments.\n\nWe’re excited about how passkeys have been adopted. And 
 while we’re continually enhancing the sign-in experience\, WhatsApp is n
 ow focused on leveraging this massive passkey adoption to secure the broad
 er application ecosystem. This presentation will explore how WhatsApp is m
 oving beyond basic authentication to unlock advanced security benefits and
  novel use cases.\n\nA key highlight of the session will be WhatsApp's ong
 oing work to integrate passkeys into re-authentication flows. Examples inc
 lude authorizing payments and allowing businesses to re-authenticate users
  within WhatsApp. This demonstrates that reducing authentication friction 
 not only helps users access the app but also fundamentally increases secur
 ity throughout their entire usage lifecycle. We have transitioned into a p
 hase where we are actively seeking use cases and security benefits that go
  beyond sign-in.\n\nHowever\, challenges remain\, particularly in regions 
 where screen lock is not prevalent\, users are not very loyal to their pas
 sword manager accounts\, and low digital literacy is a factor\, along with
  the high-friction nature of the initial password manager setup flow. We a
 re collaborating closely with industry partners to address these issues an
 d drive toward 100% passkey adoption.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com
 /event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
