BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:7a25dc1f139bc8f3959f74979e285c3dd91a8923@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T143500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T150000
SUMMARY:Who Authorized That? Proving Human Identity and Consent Behind Auto
 nomous AI Agents in Crypto Payment Flows
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Non-human Authentication\nSpeakers: Mallikarjuna Potta (
 PayPal)\; Mandar Gaonkar (PayPal)\nAI agents are transacting autonomously 
 right now — paying for APIs\, data\, and compute on behalf of their user
 s. Protocols like x402 have solved the wallet problem: every agent has a c
 ryptographically verified payment identity. But they have not solved the h
 uman problem: when an agent pays\, how does anyone know a real\, identifie
 d person authorized it?\nThis is not theoretical. The FATF Travel Rule req
 uires VASPs to transmit originator identity alongside qualifying crypto tr
 ansactions. The EU Transfer of Funds Regulation extends this to every tran
 sfer\, with no minimum threshold. Regulators are not asking which wallet p
 aid — they are asking which person authorized it. Today's agentic paymen
 t infrastructure cannot answer that question.\nThis session presents a pra
 ctical architecture built on FIDO and W3C standards. Authorization is solv
 ed with AP2 mandate credentials — W3C Verifiable Credentials signed by t
 he human at session setup\, encoding spend limits\, permitted domains\, an
 d session key delegation. Identity is solved with Verifiable Presentations
 : the human pre-delegates their KYC credential to a specific agent session
 \, scoped to a specific verifier and time window\, signed with a passkey-b
 acked key. The agent presents this autonomously per-payment. No human inte
 raction per transaction — but the credential cannot be replayed by a dif
 ferent agent\, verifier\, or after expiry.\nWallets are not homogeneous. T
 he session maps all three cases and shows how a wallet-agnostic compliance
  pipeline handles them.\nAttendees leave with a concrete architecture span
 ning passkey-backed session keys\, W3C VC/VP delegation\, AP2 mandate chai
 ns\, and Travel Rule compliance — and a clear view of what the standards
  community needs to build next.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event
 /authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Non-human Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:3921b9654ad79de85e0327d71b1240454355983e@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T143500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T150000
SUMMARY:Trust Me\, I’m Synced: Rethinking Passkey Threat Models with New 
 Attacks
LOCATION:Sol 3\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth\nSpeake
 rs: Arie Olshtein (Palo Alto Networks)\; Amir Landau (Palo Alto Networks)\
 nAttackers do not break protocols in theory. They target real-world implem
 entations\, where usability\, scale\, and complex architectures intersect.
  In this research\, we take an attacker’s perspective to analyze passkey
 s: where they reside\, how they are synchronized\, and which components ha
 ndle the most sensitive operations. We examine critical implementation det
 ails across browsers\, operating systems\, and cloud services\, areas that
  are rarely documented or publicly analyzed.\n\nFocusing on one of the mos
 t widely deployed passwordless ecosystems\, Google’s implementation of s
 ynced passkeys\, we analyze the protections designed to secure cloud-backe
 d credentials. These include enclave-based key isolation and hardware-back
 ed device keys used to gate access to cryptographic operations.\n\nWe pres
 ent new attack vectors that challenge security assumptions. By targeting s
 ynchronization flows\, recovery mechanisms\, and trust boundaries between 
 devices and cloud authenticators\, we demonstrate how attackers can achiev
 e authentication bypass and account takeover under realistic conditions.\n
 Beyond exposing weaknesses\, this talk focuses on defense. We propose prac
 tical improvements to strengthen passkey deployments\, including changes t
 o trust models\, validation flows\, and device binding. Attendees will lea
 ve with a clear understanding of emerging passkey attack surfaces and conc
 rete steps to mitigate them.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/au
 thenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Security\, Standards\, and Architecture in Depth
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:7afa69513069fff6b06ef86b6abcaaedde4beac2@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T143500
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T150000
SUMMARY:Beyond Simple Authentication for large scale Enterprises: Anchoring
  continuously verified trust with Biometric-Backed Privacy-Preserving Cred
 entials
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication\nSpeakers: 
 Aakashi Kapoor (Google)\; Paul Ambrosini (Google)\nEnterprise authenticati
 on has successfully relied on a static baseline of "what you know" and "wh
 at you have" to defeat remote credential phishing. However\, this "proof o
 f possession" model is blind to the actual human operating the device\, le
 aving organizations vulnerable to localized insider threats\, credential s
 haring\, and AI-driven attacks. To combat this\, Google’s enterprise ide
 ntity architecture must adopt an evolution from static point-in-time check
 s to continuous risk evaluation using biometric-backed\, privacy-preservin
 g credentials specifically designed for the enterprise.\n\nThis session in
 troduces the concept of a framework that dynamically correlates high-fidel
 ity trusted credentials to establish a single\, continuously verified enti
 ty for any user action. Attendees will learn how Google as an Enterprise i
 s designing an architecture that synthesizes device-bound secrets\, physic
 al access signals\, and privacy-preserving local biometrics into a unified
  risk signal for their employees. We will explore how to implement "gracef
 ul degradation" of trust\, scaling access dynamically from unmanaged low t
 rust credentials up to highly privileged\, fully managed enterprise hardwa
 re for the users of their systems. Join us as we share our learnings from 
 the ongoing experiments on how to design the next step in securing your or
 ganization and transitioning from basic remote trust to autonomous\, conti
 nuous verification trust without increasing user friction.\nAgenda: https:
 //authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:509d9c7895e44421fa14eee8d34bfa23dd747998@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T151000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T153500
SUMMARY:Identity Assurance in the Age of AI Agents
LOCATION:Sol 1\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Non-human Authentication\nSpeakers: Conor White (Daon)\n
 Passkeys have delivered a step-function improvement in phishing resistance
  and credential defense for human users. However\, as enterprises deploy a
 utonomous AI agents capable of making financial decisions\, managing workf
 lows\, and transacting on behalf of individuals\, the fundamental assumpti
 ons of identity authentication are shifting. Authenticating a human at log
 in is no longer enough when a machine holds the operational steering wheel
 .\nThis session explores the emerging realm of agentic identity and the cr
 itical frameworks required to govern autonomous actions. We will examine h
 ow to bridge the gap between human identity proofing and AI authorization\
 , moving beyond point-in-time authentication toward continuous trust decis
 ions.\nKey Takeaways:\n\n• Human vs. Machine Authentication: How to dist
 inguish between user-initiated actions and autonomous agent behavior acros
 s digital channels.\n• The Identity Assurance Stack: How passkeys\, biom
 etrics\, identity proofing\, and continuous risk assessment intersect to s
 ecure agentic workflows.\n• Trust & Accountability Frameworks: Practical
  strategies for establishing delegation limits\, auditability\, and govern
 ance for enterprise AI deployments.\n• Preparing for the Next Era: What 
 fraud\, identity\, and security leaders must build today to stay ahead of 
 AI-driven operational risk.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/aut
 henticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Non-human Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:7f7e0f7f02109dae1eadea5c5207ce9d90c387ae@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T151000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T153500
SUMMARY:From Identity Gap to Verified Agency: Securing AI Agents Beyond Aut
 hentication
LOCATION:Sol 2\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: Non-human Authentication\nSpeakers: Victor Hugo Vargas A
 rce (University of North Carolina at Charlotte)\nLarge Language Model (LLM
 ) agents are rapidly evolving from passive assistants into autonomous prin
 cipals capable of executing actions\, invoking tools\, and coordinating wi
 th other agents. This shift fundamentally challenges existing identity and
  access management models\, which were designed for human users and static
  services.\n\nThis session presents an identity-first perspective on secur
 ing agentic systems\, grounded in a recent research survey on identity\, a
 uthorization\, and trust in LLM agents. The survey identifies a systemic 
 “Identity Gap\,” where agents are granted operational authority withou
 t cryptographically enforceable identity binding\, constrained delegation 
 semantics\, or verifiable peer trust .\n\nTo address this\, we introduce a
 n “agent-as-principal” model that separates identity into two dimensio
 ns: delegation identity (how authority is transferred from users to agents
 ) and peer identity (how agents authenticate and trust each other). Using 
 this framework\, we map common failure modes—including confused deputy a
 ttacks\, identity spoofing\, and cascading multi-agent compromise—to str
 uctural weaknesses in identity and authorization rather than prompt-level 
 misalignment.\n\nBuilding on this foundation\, the session explores emergi
 ng approaches to close the gap\, including a novel direction in intent-awa
 re authorization. We present TS-PHOL\, a framework that augments tradition
 al identity and access controls by verifying whether an action is not only
  permitted but aligned with user intent.\n\nAttendees will gain a practica
 l understanding of how to extend modern identity architectures to support 
 AI agents securely\, and how identity\, delegation\, and authorization mus
 t evolve together to enable trustworthy scalable agents\nAgenda: https://a
 uthenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:Non-human Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:61c38a170d4ee039f3448a254b3acd0fd882fe87@authenticatecon.com
DTSTAMP:20261012T182052Z
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T151000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20261021T153500
SUMMARY:Becoming a Single-RP Passkey Provider: SCA-Compliant Passkeys on iO
 S
LOCATION:Sol 4\, Omni La Costa Resort & Spa\, 2100 Costa Del Mar Rd\, Carls
 bad\, CA 92009
DESCRIPTION:Track: New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication\nSpeakers: 
 Tarald Riise (STØ AS)\nLast year at Authenticate\, I shared how BankID bu
 ilt a device-bound faux-passkey experience on iOS\, delivering phishing-re
 sistant authentication to over 60% of Norway's population. To meet PSD2 SC
 A and eIDAS 2.0 requirements\, on iOS we use a biometry-protected key in t
 he Secure Enclave rather than a standard WebAuthn passkey. It works\, but 
 that key is inaccessible to the browser\, so every same-device flow on iOS
  must still send the user into the BankID app.\nI was originally going to 
 tell you how we fixed that by (ab)using the Digital Credentials API as a b
 ridge between web and native. We've since pivoted to something less exotic
  and far more promising: becoming a passkey provider on iOS. With a Creden
 tial Provider Extension\, our credential appears in the system sheet and t
 he browser receives an ordinary WebAuthn assertion. We stop pretending to 
 offer a passkey and actually offer one\, unlocking capabilities like hybri
 d transport along the way.\nHowever\, the hard problems moved rather than 
 disappeared\, and the biggest one is no longer technical. It's whether mil
 lions of Norwegians will give their de facto national eID one of the three
  credential provider slots iOS allows\, likely alongside iCloud Keychain a
 nd whatever password manager they already use. While credential providers 
 generally serve any website\, ours serves exactly one relying party: ourse
 lves.\nAgenda: https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
URL:https://authenticatecon.com/event/authenticate-u-s-2026/
TRANSP:OPAQUE
CATEGORIES:New Frontiers in Identity and Authentication
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:1aacd66a5fa5f233e2760d534a3936493cba3cde@authenticatecon.com
